00:10:15,850 --> 00:10:19,210
As we've seen in all these other
foundation modeling domains,

256
00:10:19,210 --> 00:10:22,210
these big internet scale
data sets are so diverse.

257
00:10:22,210 --> 00:10:23,110
And they're static.

258
00:10:23,110 --> 00:10:26,080
We just scrape them once or
scrape them multiple times

259
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:28,960
continuously but we
aggregate a continuous pile

260
00:10:28,960 --> 00:10:29,950
that's just growing.

261
00:10:29,950 --> 00:10:32,275
Here we see either the
pile data set from Eleuther

262
00:10:32,275 --> 00:10:36,740
or a LAION-5B for
paired image text.

263
00:10:36,740 --> 00:10:37,900
And these are pretty big.

264
00:10:37,900 --> 00:10:39,160
And they're orders
of magnitude more

265
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:40,370
than what we've seen before.

266
00:10:40,370 --> 00:10:42,037
And they are definitely
a key ingredient

267
00:10:42,037 --> 00:10:44,803
to why other domains have
been doing so well by training

268
00:10:44,803 --> 00:10:45,970
these big foundation models.

269
00:10:45,970 --> 00:10:49,900

270
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:51,430
Coming back to
robotics then, I'd

271
00:10:51,430 --> 00:10:55,060
like to take a brief detour
into how the shift came to be.

272
00:10:55,060 --> 00:10:56,950
Because it's very easy
to say in a sentence,

273
00:10:56,950 --> 00:10:58,990
yeah, robotics is
offline more than online.

274
00:10:58,990 --> 00:11:02,170
And this is coming as, kind of,
a no brainer to many folks who

275
00:11:02,170 --> 00:11:04,450
are coming from other
domains like this is the way

276
00:11:04,450 --> 00:11:05,260
things are done.

277
00:11:05,260 --> 00:11:08,510
But in robotics, this has
been a very big shift.

278
00:11:08,510 --> 00:11:10,180
And I think robotics
has also been

279
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:12,212
synonymous with
RL, reinforcement

280
00:11:12,212 --> 00:11:13,420
learning for a lot of people.

281
00:11:13,420 --> 00:11:16,460
And I think increasingly,
this is becoming less true.

282
00:11:16,460 --> 00:11:19,420
And so I'd like to take
you down a brief trip

283
00:11:19,420 --> 00:11:21,457
down the history of my team.

284
00:11:21,457 --> 00:11:23,290
The other slide of the
talk is brief history

285
00:11:23,290 --> 00:11:26,170
of robotics at Google.

286
00:11:26,170 --> 00:11:27,250
Yeah, of course, thanks.

287
00:11:27,250 --> 00:11:30,040
And I think this is not just
for dramatic exposition.

288
00:11:30,040 --> 00:11:34,120
It's really to try to guide
you through how drastically

289
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:37,150
our team's thinking has, kind
of, evolved over the years

290
00:11:37,150 --> 00:11:39,670
and how that's going
to inform the design

291
00:11:39,670 --> 00:11:43,150
decisions and the kind of
risks and research directions

292
00:11:43,150 --> 00:11:45,008
that we take in this
specific project.

293
00:11:45,008 --> 00:11:46,300
So I'm going to show coming up.

294
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:47,650
Thank you.

295
00:11:47,650 --> 00:11:50,170
So in 2016, some of
you may have seen this,

296
00:11:50,170 --> 00:11:51,970
we had what we
call the arm farm.

297
00:11:51,970 --> 00:11:56,260
Seven Kuka robots in a room,
collecting picking data 24/7.

298
00:11:56,260 --> 00:11:58,270
And this was doing on-policy RL.

299
00:11:58,270 --> 00:12:01,500
In the real world, we were the
first team to kind of say, hey,

300
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:03,040
can we even do this?

301
00:12:03,040 --> 00:12:05,860
With the goal of saying, can
we do end-to-end robot learning

302
00:12:05,860 --> 00:12:07,360
with results in the real world?

303
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:09,100
This was, kind of,
risky at the time.

304
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:10,270
It was not a common take.

305
00:12:10,270 --> 00:12:12,850
And from that we developed
several interesting research

306
00:12:12,850 --> 00:12:14,560
directions that we
started exploring.

307
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:17,570
We looked into
stuff like QT-Opt,

308
00:12:17,570 --> 00:12:21,670
which is a cue-learning method
working on continuous control

309
00:12:21,670 --> 00:12:25,000
actions while taking
vision inputs.

310
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,220
We worked on
CycleGAN to transform

311
00:12:27,220 --> 00:12:30,400
simulation-based images
into real-looking images

312
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:31,390
for [INAUDIBLE].

313
00:12:31,390 --> 00:12:32,890
We looked at
concurrent role, how

314
00:12:32,890 --> 00:12:34,540
we get robots moving
faster and more

315
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:36,160
efficiently in the real world.

316
00:12:36,160 --> 00:12:37,577
I'm sorry, do you
have a question?

317
00:12:37,577 --> 00:12:40,774
Yeah, [INAUDIBLE] 24/7 data
collection [INAUDIBLE]..

318
00:12:40,774 --> 00:12:42,670
Curious to know, how
did you read seven?

319
00:12:42,670 --> 00:12:43,870
Yeah, great question.

320
00:12:43,870 --> 00:12:47,410
And that one, I
think, was basically

321
00:12:47,410 --> 00:12:49,413
the arms would pick
stuff up from the bin.

322
00:12:49,413 --> 00:12:50,830
If they messed up
and it fell out,

323
00:12:50,830 --> 00:12:52,625
well, we'd come back
the next morning,

324
00:12:52,625 --> 00:12:55,000
and there'd be objects scattered
all throughout the room.

325
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:56,620
So there was no reset.

326
00:12:56,620 --> 00:12:58,657
But if they missed a
little bit, the objects

327
00:12:58,657 --> 00:13:00,490
would fall back into
the bin and, hopefully,

328
00:13:00,490 --> 00:13:03,088
be in a position where they
could pick them up again.

329
00:13:03,088 --> 00:13:04,630
I wanted to ask you
if you can repeat

330
00:13:04,630 --> 00:13:05,755
the question they've asked.

331
00:13:05,755 --> 00:13:07,190
Oh, yeah, of course.

332
00:13:07,190 --> 00:13:07,690
Thanks.

333
00:13:07,690 --> 00:13:08,815
I'll do that in the future.

334
00:13:08,815 --> 00:13:11,560
This specific question was,
for this 24/7 arm farm,

335
00:13:11,560 --> 00:13:13,270
how did we do resets?

336
00:13:13,270 --> 00:13:15,520
And the answer is,
well, we didn't.

337
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:17,140
We designed the bin
so that they were

338
00:13:17,140 --> 00:13:19,150
banked so that if an
object slightly missed,

339
00:13:19,150 --> 00:13:21,857
they would fall back in the bin,
reorient themselves, maybe add

340
00:13:21,857 --> 00:13:23,440
more diversity with
the training data.

341
00:13:23,440 --> 00:13:25,750
But this was doing
off-policy online RL

342
00:13:25,750 --> 00:13:27,700
row with cue learning.

343
00:13:27,700 --> 00:13:29,950
And we mixed it with same
data, deploy it again.

344
00:13:29,950 --> 00:13:32,630

345
00:13:32,630 --> 00:13:36,552
Next, we went through this
consolidation phase around 2020

346
00:13:36,552 --> 00:13:37,760
when we were like, all right.

347
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:39,703
This is pretty cool.

348
00:13:39,703 --> 00:13:41,120
But we want to get
out of the bin.

349
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:44,545
How do we do more complex tasks
in a more practical setting

350
00:13:44,545 --> 00:13:45,920
that could be
closer to something

351
00:13:45,920 --> 00:13:48,770
that humans would want to use,
that's more general every day?

352
00:13:48,770 --> 00:13:51,890
There, we settled on this office
micro kitchen environment,

353
00:13:51,890 --> 00:13:55,010
if you've heard of the
famous Google micro-kitchens.

354
00:13:55,010 --> 00:13:58,700
And I think this was the setting
we decided to operate in.

355
00:13:58,700 --> 00:14:00,590
And there, we started
collecting data.

356
00:14:00,590 --> 00:14:02,090
We scaled our operations.

357
00:14:02,090 --> 00:14:04,830
And there we scaled approaches
to some different things.

358
00:14:04,830 --> 00:14:06,680
And I think, in the
bottom right here,

359
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:09,480
is the more mechanized
reset version, I would say,

360
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:10,670
of the arm farm.

361
00:14:10,670 --> 00:14:12,950
Here, we had a bin
that folded in half.

362
00:14:12,950 --> 00:14:15,145
And this was doing multitask
RL in the real world.

363
00:14:15,145 --> 00:14:17,270
And the bin would flipping
in half, dumping objects

364
00:14:17,270 --> 00:14:18,270
from one side to the other.

365
00:14:18,270 --> 00:14:19,640
So you could do more
interesting tasks,

366
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:21,530
whereas the arm farm
was, pick anything up.

367
00:14:21,530 --> 00:14:23,300
Now we could say, hey,
pick up the carrot

368
00:14:23,300 --> 00:14:26,240
and place the tomato
onto the plate.

369
00:14:26,240 --> 00:14:28,490
And then the bin would
flip, and you'd reset.

370
00:14:28,490 --> 00:14:30,608
Some other works we
look at multitasking

371
00:14:30,608 --> 00:14:31,400
imitation learning.

372
00:14:31,400 --> 00:14:32,440
This is BC-zero.

373
00:14:32,440 --> 00:14:34,940
And then, we also look at stuff
like combining reinforcement

374
00:14:34,940 --> 00:14:39,180
learning with imitation learning
bootstrapping [INAUDIBLE]..

375
00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:42,630
But in 2020, once
again, we realized

376
00:14:42,630 --> 00:14:45,030
we were working in a ton
of different directions,

377
00:14:45,030 --> 00:14:46,470
and we wanted to consolidate.

378
00:14:46,470 --> 00:14:49,290
And I think the two main things
that were really bothering us

379
00:14:49,290 --> 00:14:52,260
at the point, at the time, were
we are hitting two main walls

380
00:14:52,260 --> 00:14:53,610
across all these methods.

381
00:14:53,610 --> 00:14:57,690
Some of them were plateauing
at this 50% to 70%

382
00:14:57,690 --> 00:14:59,580
rough range in the real world.

383
00:14:59,580 --> 00:15:02,670
And other methods were
requiring very specific data

384
00:15:02,670 --> 00:15:03,540
distributions.

385
00:15:03,540 --> 00:15:05,430
They had to be on policy,
or they could only

386
00:15:05,430 --> 00:15:07,680
use demonstrations, or
they blah, blah, blah.

387
00:15:07,680 --> 00:15:10,055
There were so many different
nuances and gotchas

388
00:15:10,055 --> 00:15:11,430
to all of these
different methods

389
00:15:11,430 --> 00:15:13,030
and all of these
different drawbacks.

390
00:15:13,030 --> 00:15:15,060
And so the question
we posed was,

391
00:15:15,060 --> 00:15:18,480
we're open to any
method, any strategy that

392
00:15:18,480 --> 00:15:21,105
will enable us to solve tasks
in a very performant matter

393
00:15:21,105 --> 00:15:23,430
manner, more than 90%
in the real world,

394
00:15:23,430 --> 00:15:26,850
and, also, that can scale
with some kind of data

395
00:15:26,850 --> 00:15:28,290
that we can collect.

396
00:15:28,290 --> 00:15:31,360
And maybe this is a bit
more lax than, let's say,

397
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:34,200
an academic setting where you're
much more resource-constrained.

398
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:36,510
But at the end of the day,
even our team does not

399
00:15:36,510 --> 00:15:38,640
have infinite money, we
still have a certain number

400
00:15:38,640 --> 00:15:40,380
of robots, a certain
number of operators,

401
00:15:40,380 --> 00:15:42,190
and we're constrained
by the laws of physics.

402
00:15:42,190 --> 00:15:43,857
So we need some way
to acquire more data

403
00:15:43,857 --> 00:15:45,190
that we can then learn from.

404
00:15:45,190 --> 00:15:46,732
And so we're all
scratching our heads

405
00:15:46,732 --> 00:15:50,100
thinking about this for a
few months in spring 2022.

406
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:53,290
We decided on going with
multi-task imitation learning.

407
00:15:53,290 --> 00:15:56,340
So this was a vast departure
from the 24/7 arm farm.

408
00:15:56,340 --> 00:15:59,860
This was a vast evolution of
how we approach the problem.

409
00:15:59,860 --> 00:16:03,150
We found that with enough,
no gentle care and love,

410
00:16:03,150 --> 00:16:04,560
multi-task imitation
learning was

411
00:16:04,560 --> 00:16:06,420
able to hit these 90% numbers.

412
00:16:06,420 --> 00:16:09,210
And it was able to get better
with more demonstrations.

413
00:16:09,210 --> 00:16:11,280
These aren't the
cheapest thing, but it

414
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:13,560
was able to scale with
additional demonstrations,

415
00:16:13,560 --> 00:16:16,150
which was a sign of life
that we were looking for.

416
00:16:16,150 --> 00:16:19,050
So that brings us to
less than a year ago,

417
00:16:19,050 --> 00:16:21,190
our team was deciding
this is the path forward,

418
00:16:21,190 --> 00:16:22,740
at least in the
near-term future,

419
00:16:22,740 --> 00:16:27,540
but maybe, we could
just think about how

420
00:16:27,540 --> 00:16:30,608
the approach we were
taking here might also

421
00:16:30,608 --> 00:16:31,650
spread out in the future.

422
00:16:31,650 --> 00:16:34,140
And we might be able to bring
back these other threads.

423
00:16:34,140 --> 00:16:37,680
For example, if now that we're
decoupling this data collection

424
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:40,530
of demonstrations, or
et cetera, from how

425
00:16:40,530 --> 00:16:42,870
we learn from them with a
multitask imitation learning

426
00:16:42,870 --> 00:16:44,400
policy, maybe we
can, in the future,

427
00:16:44,400 --> 00:16:46,710
then do something
like offline RL.

428
00:16:46,710 --> 00:16:48,780
But I think at a
high level now--

429
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:51,180
I've just, in a
few short minutes,

430
00:16:51,180 --> 00:16:53,957
just compressed six years
of very bitter lessons

431
00:16:53,957 --> 00:16:55,290
that our team has been learning.

432
00:16:55,290 --> 00:16:57,072
And I think, from
where we are today,

433
00:16:57,072 --> 00:16:58,780
and looking back even
just two years ago,

434
00:16:58,780 --> 00:17:00,870
if you told me that the
strategies we're deploying

435
00:17:00,870 --> 00:17:02,830
today could just scale
the way they are,

436
00:17:02,830 --> 00:17:05,256
I probably would not
have believed you.

437
00:17:05,256 --> 00:17:06,089
You have a question?

438
00:17:06,089 --> 00:17:14,099
[INAUDIBLE] instead of
trying to figure out

439
00:17:14,099 --> 00:17:15,960
how [INAUDIBLE] data sets.

440
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:16,720
Great question.

441
00:17:16,720 --> 00:17:19,470
So I think task conditioning
is definitely still-- was

442
00:17:19,470 --> 00:17:21,270
an open question at the time.

443
00:17:21,270 --> 00:17:24,030
But I think with
this work, BC-zero,

444
00:17:24,030 --> 00:17:27,180
we found that language
was able, at least

445
00:17:27,180 --> 00:17:30,277
in a templated language
representation,

446
00:17:30,277 --> 00:17:31,860
was good enough where
we could direct.

447
00:17:31,860 --> 00:17:33,570
I think BC-zero
is over 80 tasks.

448
00:17:33,570 --> 00:17:38,100
So they were very templated,
like pick grapes, or move

449
00:17:38,100 --> 00:17:41,530
grapes onto a plate,
or drag this across--

450
00:17:41,530 --> 00:17:43,080
drag a cloth across a table.

451
00:17:43,080 --> 00:17:45,600
And I think this
representation was still enough

452
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:47,708
where you're learning a
good number of skills.

453
00:17:47,708 --> 00:17:50,250
You're passing in, essentially,
a one-hot ID into your policy

454
00:17:50,250 --> 00:17:50,790
network.

455
00:17:50,790 --> 00:17:52,270
And it will learn
to imitate that.

456
00:17:52,270 --> 00:17:53,728
And for each one
of those 80 tasks,

457
00:17:53,728 --> 00:17:57,060
it'd collect hundreds or
thousands of the imitations.

458
00:17:57,060 --> 00:17:59,440
And we'll touch upon the
specifics of that a bit later,

459
00:17:59,440 --> 00:17:59,940
too.

460
00:17:59,940 --> 00:18:03,510

461
00:18:03,510 --> 00:18:08,910
So yeah, today, or at least in
2022, let's do offline methods.

462
00:18:08,910 --> 00:18:12,060
Let's decouple data generation
from data consumption.

463
00:18:12,060 --> 00:18:14,030
And let's take these
three lessons now

464
00:18:14,030 --> 00:18:15,030
that we've touched upon.

465
00:18:15,030 --> 00:18:17,145
Let's take the design
principles of ML scaling,

466
00:18:17,145 --> 00:18:19,020
and then figure out what
lessons can actually

467
00:18:19,020 --> 00:18:21,300
be applied when you go--
look into the future

468
00:18:21,300 --> 00:18:26,020
for a recipe for robot
learning and foundation models.

469
00:18:26,020 --> 00:18:28,020
The first lesson I think
is very important is

470
00:18:28,020 --> 00:18:30,233
this high-capacity
architecture, is like attention.

471
00:18:30,233 --> 00:18:31,650
The second, I'll
touch upon later,

472
00:18:31,650 --> 00:18:36,300
is data interoperability,
tokenization, discretization.

473
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:38,730
And the second ingredient
is the proliferation

474
00:18:38,730 --> 00:18:40,170
of these models themselves.

475
00:18:40,170 --> 00:18:43,630
Can we leverage them because
they will get better over time?

476
00:18:43,630 --> 00:18:45,270
And I think here, I
would like to plug

477
00:18:45,270 --> 00:18:48,130
my colleague Karol
Hausman's bitter lesson 2.0,

478
00:18:48,130 --> 00:18:49,660
which is the bitter lesson.

479
00:18:49,660 --> 00:18:51,120
The first one from
Richard Sutton

480
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:54,390
was, you should leverage methods
that scale with more compute.

481
00:18:54,390 --> 00:18:56,850
And maybe in
today's day and age,

482
00:18:56,850 --> 00:18:58,800
the lesson is that we
should leverage methods

483
00:18:58,800 --> 00:19:01,170
that are able to
utilize improvements

484
00:19:01,170 --> 00:19:03,600
in foundation models because
they're going to get better.

485
00:19:03,600 --> 00:19:04,440
Yeah.

486
00:19:04,440 --> 00:19:06,858
So both in the bitter
lesson 1.0 and 2.0,

487
00:19:06,858 --> 00:19:08,400
one thing that's
always unclear to me

488
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:10,748
is suppose I have
a set of methods,

489
00:19:10,748 --> 00:19:12,290
and I want to choose
the methods that

490
00:19:12,290 --> 00:19:14,540
are going to scale with more
compute or, in this case,

491
00:19:14,540 --> 00:19:16,400
scale with better
foundation models.

492
00:19:16,400 --> 00:19:18,290
The question is,
how do I actually

493
00:19:18,290 --> 00:19:22,610
decide which of those
methods meets those criteria?

494
00:19:22,610 --> 00:19:23,760
Yeah, a great question.

495
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:26,930
I think, and maybe it's--
and I think that's a very--

496
00:19:26,930 --> 00:19:28,430
I don't have a good
answer for that.

497
00:19:28,430 --> 00:19:29,120
Could you repeat the question?

498
00:19:29,120 --> 00:19:30,020
Oh, sorry, yeah.

499
00:19:30,020 --> 00:19:32,840
The question was in bitter
lesson 1.0 and bitter lesson

500
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:34,670
2.0, the question is,
well, that's great.

501
00:19:34,670 --> 00:19:35,420
That's the lesson.

502
00:19:35,420 --> 00:19:37,550
But how do we
actually decide which

503
00:19:37,550 --> 00:19:39,020
methods meet these criteria?

504
00:19:39,020 --> 00:19:42,630
And I think my answer is
it's not always obvious.

505
00:19:42,630 --> 00:19:44,370
And it's actually
quite tricky sometimes.

506
00:19:44,370 --> 00:19:48,320
But maybe, sometimes what-- you
can be very confident that, Oh,

507
00:19:48,320 --> 00:19:50,840
yeah, this will definitely scale
with more data and compute.

508
00:19:50,840 --> 00:19:52,070
And some that are the same--

509
00:19:52,070 --> 00:19:53,610
basically, the more
hard-coded you are,

510
00:19:53,610 --> 00:19:56,068
the more assumptions, the more
heuristics you make bake in,

511
00:19:56,068 --> 00:19:57,530
the more you-- in
our day and age,

512
00:19:57,530 --> 00:20:00,230
the more you rely on a
specific implementation

513
00:20:00,230 --> 00:20:03,350
of a specific foundation
model of a specific class

514
00:20:03,350 --> 00:20:06,860
of algorithm maybe that will be
less robust than a method that

515
00:20:06,860 --> 00:20:10,160
just assumes some very abstract
input and output and assumes

516
00:20:10,160 --> 00:20:12,080
that how you get from
that input and output

517
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:13,220
can improve over time.

518
00:20:13,220 --> 00:20:16,200
And maybe the algorithm itself
even changes altogether.

519
00:20:16,200 --> 00:20:18,950
So I think that would be my
take on the bitter lesson 2.0.

520
00:20:18,950 --> 00:20:20,930
But this is definitely still--

521
00:20:20,930 --> 00:20:25,200
I think the jury's
still out on this.

522
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:29,340
And my basic-- one of the
things I like to propose

523
00:20:29,340 --> 00:20:31,080
is that language
is the way that we

524
00:20:31,080 --> 00:20:32,820
can leverage bitter lesson 2.0.

525
00:20:32,820 --> 00:20:35,257
If you have language as the
universal representation

526
00:20:35,257 --> 00:20:36,840
through which all
of these foundations

527
00:20:36,840 --> 00:20:39,720
communicate to each other,
whether it's captioning,

528
00:20:39,720 --> 00:20:41,550
or generation, or
whatnot, I think

529
00:20:41,550 --> 00:20:47,030
that's one way that we could
leverage bitter lessons 2.0.

530
00:20:47,030 --> 00:20:48,940
And finally, the
third ingredient,

531
00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:51,670
offline robot learning,
decoupling data generation

532
00:20:51,670 --> 00:20:53,350
from data consumption.

533
00:20:53,350 --> 00:20:56,080
Putting these all
together, my recipe

534
00:20:56,080 --> 00:20:58,612
for how one takes
at a modern attempt

535
00:20:58,612 --> 00:21:00,070
at embodied
intelligence would look

536
00:21:00,070 --> 00:21:03,100
like would be to combine
these large offline data

537
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:05,380
sets with high-capacity
architectures

538
00:21:05,380 --> 00:21:08,080
by using language as
the universal glue.

539
00:21:08,080 --> 00:21:10,510
And in the works I'm
going to present shortly,

540
00:21:10,510 --> 00:21:13,690
all of our different projects,
I think, in some way or another,

541
00:21:13,690 --> 00:21:16,270
are inspired by this philosophy.

542
00:21:16,270 --> 00:21:19,670

543
00:21:19,670 --> 00:21:24,440
And now, that we've understood
the motivations and potentially

544
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:25,690
one possible approach--

545
00:21:25,690 --> 00:21:26,190
Sorry.

546
00:21:26,190 --> 00:21:27,273
Can you go back one slide?

547
00:21:27,273 --> 00:21:27,936
Of course.

548
00:21:27,936 --> 00:21:29,200
The bottom, the recipe--

549
00:21:29,200 --> 00:21:30,880
"large diverse offline data
sets with high-capacity

550
00:21:30,880 --> 00:21:33,005
architectures using language
as a universal glue"--

551
00:21:33,005 --> 00:21:37,483
I'm curious to know which, if
any, of these are currently--

552
00:21:37,483 --> 00:21:38,900
[INAUDIBLE] is not
the right word.

553
00:21:38,900 --> 00:21:40,640
Which of these are limited?

554
00:21:40,640 --> 00:21:41,360
Got it.

555
00:21:41,360 --> 00:21:43,160
Because it seems to me like we
already have large offline data

556
00:21:43,160 --> 00:21:43,660
sets.

557
00:21:43,660 --> 00:21:45,200
We have high-capacity
architectures.

558
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:47,282
Those architectures
[INAUDIBLE] language.

559
00:21:47,282 --> 00:21:49,740
It seems like we already have
all the components necessary.

560
00:21:49,740 --> 00:21:53,540
So why is this then
not a solved problem?

561
00:21:53,540 --> 00:21:55,970
The question was, it
seems like we have

562
00:21:55,970 --> 00:21:57,110
a lot of these ingredients.

563
00:21:57,110 --> 00:22:00,470
And so, why hasn't
robotics been solved yet?

564
00:22:00,470 --> 00:22:03,767
So I would argue that actually,
this take here, and maybe I'm--

565
00:22:03,767 --> 00:22:05,600
this is to the wrong
audience at the moment.

566
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:07,580
But I think this is
not very non-obvious

567
00:22:07,580 --> 00:22:08,930
across the robotics field.

568
00:22:08,930 --> 00:22:11,120
Many people do
not agree with all

569
00:22:11,120 --> 00:22:14,940
of these, much less two of these
or even any of these points.

570
00:22:14,940 --> 00:22:17,360
And so I think-- and
also the existence

571
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:20,600
of the scale of how mature
each of these components

572
00:22:20,600 --> 00:22:23,083
are within robotics is
that very different stages.

573
00:22:23,083 --> 00:22:25,250
And I would say-- and we
can talk a bit later about,

574
00:22:25,250 --> 00:22:27,920
for example, data scale
or the architectures that

575
00:22:27,920 --> 00:22:32,120
have diffused through
osmosis from other ML domains

576
00:22:32,120 --> 00:22:34,070
into robotics, but
I think we're still

577
00:22:34,070 --> 00:22:36,563
at very different
stages on how much

578
00:22:36,563 --> 00:22:38,480
people have actually
bought into these lessons

579
00:22:38,480 --> 00:22:39,537
and invested in them.

580
00:22:39,537 --> 00:22:40,870
Can I have a follow-up question?

581
00:22:40,870 --> 00:22:41,370
Yeah.

582
00:22:41,370 --> 00:22:42,620
[INAUDIBLE]

583
00:22:42,620 --> 00:22:43,880
I'm curious to know--

584
00:22:43,880 --> 00:22:46,520
I'm not asking you to name
names, but the people who

585
00:22:46,520 --> 00:22:52,180
might not be buying into all
these pieces [INAUDIBLE]..

586
00:22:52,180 --> 00:22:54,470
Yeah, I can probably--

587
00:22:54,470 --> 00:22:57,430
I also don't want to get
into too much trouble here.

588
00:22:57,430 --> 00:22:59,030
I'll probably get
myself in a bit

589
00:22:59,030 --> 00:23:00,360
of hot water in a few slides.

590
00:23:00,360 --> 00:23:02,415
So I'll expand
upon it a bit then.

591
00:23:02,415 --> 00:23:02,915
Sounds good.

592
00:23:02,915 --> 00:23:03,290
Yeah.

593
00:23:03,290 --> 00:23:03,990
I'm just curious to know
what their opinion is

594
00:23:03,990 --> 00:23:05,948
and why you think they're
wrong on the subject.

595
00:23:05,948 --> 00:23:07,110
[INAUDIBLE]

596
00:23:07,110 --> 00:23:07,610
Yeah.

597
00:23:07,610 --> 00:23:11,770
And I would say that
me, personally, and--

598
00:23:11,770 --> 00:23:14,270
I'm not speaking for my team,
but a lot of people on my team

599
00:23:14,270 --> 00:23:18,230
are probably at the very extreme
end of learning, scaling,

600
00:23:18,230 --> 00:23:22,932
data-driven, foundation
model-based, let's go big.

601
00:23:22,932 --> 00:23:24,890
And I think a lot of
people don't believe that,

602
00:23:24,890 --> 00:23:28,200
and, yeah, happy to discuss
why later, maybe after the Zoom

603
00:23:28,200 --> 00:23:28,700
as well.

604
00:23:28,700 --> 00:23:31,085
[LAUGHTER]

605
00:23:31,085 --> 00:23:34,400
So yeah, well, OK, then
let's go ahead and dive in

606
00:23:34,400 --> 00:23:37,250
and see how this recipe
might actually percolate

607
00:23:37,250 --> 00:23:39,770
into specific domains.

608
00:23:39,770 --> 00:23:42,470
And the first one is RT-1.

609
00:23:42,470 --> 00:23:44,150
This is a recent
work from our group

610
00:23:44,150 --> 00:23:47,120
that works on how we can
scale imitation learning.

611
00:23:47,120 --> 00:23:48,830
And let's look at
how we can actually

612
00:23:48,830 --> 00:23:50,700
apply these first principles.

613
00:23:50,700 --> 00:23:54,470
So the first one is to
consider what we actually--

614
00:23:54,470 --> 00:23:56,990
let's put ourselves into
the spring 2020 mindset.

615
00:23:56,990 --> 00:23:59,120
We've been collecting
demonstrations for a while.

616
00:23:59,120 --> 00:24:02,000
This is a ton of
demos, like 100,000.

617
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,460
Data was collected
over a year and a half

618
00:24:04,460 --> 00:24:07,220
on many, many robots
on many, many tasks.

619
00:24:07,220 --> 00:24:08,040
That exists.

620
00:24:08,040 --> 00:24:09,020
It was expensive.

621
00:24:09,020 --> 00:24:11,690
And over time, this
will actually not

622
00:24:11,690 --> 00:24:13,580
trickle up at insane amounts.

623
00:24:13,580 --> 00:24:16,700
We won't just get 100,000 new
high-quality demos every day.

624
00:24:16,700 --> 00:24:18,410
This will grow over
time, but it's not

625
00:24:18,410 --> 00:24:20,720
going to grow for free.

626
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:22,220
And autonomous
ways of doing this

627
00:24:22,220 --> 00:24:24,020
is very hard, as you
saw earlier with ntop

628
00:24:24,020 --> 00:24:26,138
with the bin reset
mechanism, or DeepMind

629
00:24:26,138 --> 00:24:27,680
has a record
RGB-stacking, where they

630
00:24:27,680 --> 00:24:29,120
try to do autonomous resets.

631
00:24:29,120 --> 00:24:32,120
And the way that we're doing
it right now, or at least

632
00:24:32,120 --> 00:24:34,760
for this paper, was
human teleoperation

633
00:24:34,760 --> 00:24:36,260
pioneered by BC-zero.

634
00:24:36,260 --> 00:24:38,380
And that was very
expensive as well.

635
00:24:38,380 --> 00:24:40,130
So there's going to
be limited throughput.

636
00:24:40,130 --> 00:24:43,017
And finally, BC-zero used
a ResNet base backbone.

637
00:24:43,017 --> 00:24:44,600
And it was pretty
good, but they found

638
00:24:44,600 --> 00:24:46,920
that it was very sensitive
to training distributions.

639
00:24:46,920 --> 00:24:49,760
For example, when they remove
data from some teleoperators

640
00:24:49,760 --> 00:24:51,380
to make the data
more homogeneous,

641
00:24:51,380 --> 00:24:52,640
performance got better.

642
00:24:52,640 --> 00:24:54,390
And that's not really
a property we like.

643
00:24:54,390 --> 00:24:57,870
We want more data, even if
it's not exactly the same.

644
00:24:57,870 --> 00:25:00,980
So the lesson here,
models need to be robust,

645
00:25:00,980 --> 00:25:02,550
and they need to generalize.

646
00:25:02,550 --> 00:25:03,050
Cool.

647
00:25:03,050 --> 00:25:05,600
So we have, models need to
be robust and generalized.

648
00:25:05,600 --> 00:25:06,478
What else do we have?

649
00:25:06,478 --> 00:25:08,270
Well, off-the-shelf
models are pretty slow.

650
00:25:08,270 --> 00:25:10,970
If we take in these
huge vision transformers

651
00:25:10,970 --> 00:25:13,880
from other domains, they're not
going to run on the real robot.

652
00:25:13,880 --> 00:25:16,648
We need to be able to run
at a pretty high frequency.

653
00:25:16,648 --> 00:25:17,690
They need to be reactive.

654
00:25:17,690 --> 00:25:20,300
Inference time needs to be
slow because all our models are

655
00:25:20,300 --> 00:25:22,180
vision-based.

656
00:25:22,180 --> 00:25:25,900
And finally, we want our data to
be able to understand language.

657
00:25:25,900 --> 00:25:28,090
As I mentioned, if language
is the universal glue,

658
00:25:28,090 --> 00:25:29,920
our data set already
has some language.

659
00:25:29,920 --> 00:25:32,740
And we want eventual models
to be very multi-modal.

660
00:25:32,740 --> 00:25:35,630
This is first principle
that we need to bake in.

661
00:25:35,630 --> 00:25:36,570
What does this mean?

662
00:25:36,570 --> 00:25:38,153
We can't just take
something existing.

663
00:25:38,153 --> 00:25:40,490
We probably need to design
or at least modify something

664
00:25:40,490 --> 00:25:41,330
from the ground up.

665
00:25:41,330 --> 00:25:43,880
And let's take the best
practices that we've

666
00:25:43,880 --> 00:25:47,100
seen work in other fields.

667
00:25:47,100 --> 00:25:48,930
And so we worked for a bit.

668
00:25:48,930 --> 00:25:52,440
And we came up with this
architecture for RT-1.

669
00:25:52,440 --> 00:25:54,120
Again, once again,
this is a large team

670
00:25:54,120 --> 00:25:55,930
with a bunch of
different contributions.

671
00:25:55,930 --> 00:25:58,830
And I'll just go through
a few of them here.

672
00:25:58,830 --> 00:26:02,430
At a high level, RT-1
is robotics transformer.

673
00:26:02,430 --> 00:26:03,780
It operates at three hertz.

674
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:06,570
It takes in visual
input from the robot,

675
00:26:06,570 --> 00:26:10,410
or RGB camera, as well as
natural language instruction.

676
00:26:10,410 --> 00:26:13,320
There the image is
petrified and fed

677
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:16,320
into a film-EfficientNet
tokenizer.

678
00:26:16,320 --> 00:26:18,060
It's then passed
into token learning,

679
00:26:18,060 --> 00:26:19,720
which I'll talk about soon.

680
00:26:19,720 --> 00:26:22,830
And then also the language
instructions are tokenized.

681
00:26:22,830 --> 00:26:25,170
And then, they are put
into the same transformer.

682
00:26:25,170 --> 00:26:29,580
And then, finally, we output the
discretized actions as tokens

683
00:26:29,580 --> 00:26:32,100
and send that to the real world
in three hertz in a closed

684
00:26:32,100 --> 00:26:32,600
loop.

685
00:26:32,600 --> 00:26:35,100

686
00:26:35,100 --> 00:26:37,050
This transformer
is a decoder one.

687
00:26:37,050 --> 00:26:39,810
We use a sparse categorical
entropy objective

688
00:26:39,810 --> 00:26:43,230
for action prediction by
applying a causal mask.

689
00:26:43,230 --> 00:26:45,690
We use the pre-trained
EfficientNet backbone.

690
00:26:45,690 --> 00:26:47,790
And we also use token
learner for very--

691
00:26:47,790 --> 00:26:49,680
for faster inference.

692
00:26:49,680 --> 00:26:50,970
Diving a little bit deeper--

693
00:26:50,970 --> 00:26:51,510
Oh, sorry.

694
00:26:51,510 --> 00:26:52,218
Yeah, a question?

695
00:26:52,218 --> 00:26:53,072
[INAUDIBLE]?

696
00:26:53,072 --> 00:26:58,440

697
00:26:58,440 --> 00:26:59,460
Great question.

698
00:26:59,460 --> 00:27:03,630
So the image token, when it
goes in from-- so each image

699
00:27:03,630 --> 00:27:07,350
is the high-fidelity RGB
image from the camera.

700
00:27:07,350 --> 00:27:10,080
We split that up into
81 separate patches.

701
00:27:10,080 --> 00:27:11,520
And so each patch is--

702
00:27:11,520 --> 00:27:14,580
it's spatially just
like the square there.

703
00:27:14,580 --> 00:27:17,790
But the cool thing is that
what TokenLearner does here,

704
00:27:17,790 --> 00:27:22,140
this thing here, is-- it's a
previous work from our group

705
00:27:22,140 --> 00:27:25,950
that takes in a bunch of
possible image patches

706
00:27:25,950 --> 00:27:29,130
and dynamically selects
which of those image

707
00:27:29,130 --> 00:27:31,950
patch tokens are more
relevant for the task at hand

708
00:27:31,950 --> 00:27:33,670
given the existing context.

709
00:27:33,670 --> 00:27:36,090
So from those 81
image patch tokens,

710
00:27:36,090 --> 00:27:39,570
we sub-sample eight of
them to use for inference.

711
00:27:39,570 --> 00:27:42,267
And this happens at
every time stamp.

712
00:27:42,267 --> 00:27:44,600
And that process has learned,
which of the eight patches

713
00:27:44,600 --> 00:27:46,820
are relevant at
any given moment.

714
00:27:46,820 --> 00:27:50,708
And otherwise, we're sending
in way too many tokens.

715
00:27:50,708 --> 00:27:52,250
And the context
length would explode.

716
00:27:52,250 --> 00:27:54,333
And we wouldn't be able
to do inference on robots.

717
00:27:54,333 --> 00:27:58,137
We are also passing in a single
sequence length of six images.

718
00:27:58,137 --> 00:27:59,720
History is quite
important when you're

719
00:27:59,720 --> 00:28:02,780
doing temporally coherent
tasks in the real world where

720
00:28:02,780 --> 00:28:06,680
things like physics and exactly
this nuanced detail of what

721
00:28:06,680 --> 00:28:08,660
the objects are doing in
relation to each other

722
00:28:08,660 --> 00:28:12,180
and to your robot those
details really matter.

723
00:28:12,180 --> 00:28:17,010
And in total, the model size
is 35 million parameters,

724
00:28:17,010 --> 00:28:18,770
which is quite a bit
smaller than a lot

725
00:28:18,770 --> 00:28:22,370
of these other huge
internet-scale models.

726
00:28:22,370 --> 00:28:26,360
And finally, one main difference
here is action democratization.

727
00:28:26,360 --> 00:28:29,810
Before a lot of the products,
we are doing continuous control.

728
00:28:29,810 --> 00:28:32,480
And if you think about
it, our robot does have--

729
00:28:32,480 --> 00:28:35,330
We do end effector post
control or position control.

730
00:28:35,330 --> 00:28:38,960
And they are-- the real world
is a continuous state space.

731
00:28:38,960 --> 00:28:41,210
And to do that,
we had to come up

732
00:28:41,210 --> 00:28:45,530
with many algorithmic novelties,
for example, a CEM actor that

733
00:28:45,530 --> 00:28:48,387
did basically sampling of
these continuous action spaces

734
00:28:48,387 --> 00:28:49,970
to propose the highest
ones that would

735
00:28:49,970 --> 00:28:51,440
get rated by the Q function.

736
00:28:51,440 --> 00:28:53,120
And we do this twice,
blah, blah, blah.

737
00:28:53,120 --> 00:28:54,710
But that's so sensitive.

738
00:28:54,710 --> 00:28:56,840
But we needed to do that
to get things to work.

739
00:28:56,840 --> 00:29:00,470
But now, we just decided,
let's just bin our actions.

740
00:29:00,470 --> 00:29:03,230
It's only 256 discrete actions.

741
00:29:03,230 --> 00:29:05,780
And let's just predict
those as tokens.

742
00:29:05,780 --> 00:29:06,493
Any questions?

743
00:29:06,493 --> 00:29:07,910
Yeah, what I was
going to ask is--

744
00:29:07,910 --> 00:29:11,200
so I heard you mentioning
that you have this design

745
00:29:11,200 --> 00:29:13,600
requirement or engineering
requirement about speed,

746
00:29:13,600 --> 00:29:15,268
latency reaction.

747
00:29:15,268 --> 00:29:16,810
I think we say that
that necessitates

748
00:29:16,810 --> 00:29:19,220
having a relatively small
model, which makes sense.

749
00:29:19,220 --> 00:29:20,650
But one of the
lessons of scaling,

750
00:29:20,650 --> 00:29:22,442
when we're talking
about foundation models,

751
00:29:22,442 --> 00:29:25,642
is that [INAUDIBLE] bottleneck
by either data or parameters.

752
00:29:25,642 --> 00:29:27,100
So I guess what
I'm curious to know

753
00:29:27,100 --> 00:29:29,020
is how do you balance
these off in the sense

754
