1004
00:39:14,500 --> 00:39:16,390
is for what happens
when you reduce

1005
00:39:16,390 --> 00:39:18,160
the total number of tasks.

1006
00:39:18,160 --> 00:39:20,200
And we found that
having more tasks

1007
00:39:20,200 --> 00:39:22,930
is relatively more
important than having

1008
00:39:22,930 --> 00:39:25,270
more data for each task.

1009
00:39:25,270 --> 00:39:28,090
And I think this was a lesson
that I think is probably

1010
00:39:28,090 --> 00:39:31,720
going to suggest ways that
we should scale robotics

1011
00:39:31,720 --> 00:39:32,470
even further.

1012
00:39:32,470 --> 00:39:35,230
It's not to just collect
more data of the same task

1013
00:39:35,230 --> 00:39:37,510
in the same settings but
to go out into the wild

1014
00:39:37,510 --> 00:39:40,122
and get more diverse behavior.

1015
00:39:40,122 --> 00:39:41,520
Quick question.

1016
00:39:41,520 --> 00:39:43,775
How do you define
diversity in the data?

1017
00:39:43,775 --> 00:39:44,807
Great question.

1018
00:39:44,807 --> 00:39:46,890
The question is, how do
you define data diversity?

1019
00:39:46,890 --> 00:39:49,670
In this case, it's
just a number of

1020
00:39:49,670 --> 00:39:51,590
unique structure-templated
commands

1021
00:39:51,590 --> 00:39:53,390
that teleoperators receive.

1022
00:39:53,390 --> 00:39:56,990
So those 700 templated commands,
when we start reducing them

1023
00:39:56,990 --> 00:40:00,260
and only train on 500 or
only trained on 300 of them,

1024
00:40:00,260 --> 00:40:02,190
performance drops much
quicker than if we

1025
00:40:02,190 --> 00:40:06,210
had taken the same proportional
cuts to the total number.

1026
00:40:06,210 --> 00:40:06,710
Yeah.

1027
00:40:06,710 --> 00:40:07,210
OK.

1028
00:40:07,210 --> 00:40:09,086
So I guess I'm very [INAUDIBLE].

1029
00:40:09,086 --> 00:40:14,300

1030
00:40:14,300 --> 00:40:17,705
It seems almost [INAUDIBLE]
tipping point [INAUDIBLE]..

1031
00:40:17,705 --> 00:40:22,430

1032
00:40:22,430 --> 00:40:24,313
Yeah, I don't think we--

1033
00:40:24,313 --> 00:40:25,730
the question was
there seems to be

1034
00:40:25,730 --> 00:40:28,510
almost a linear correlation
between a data size and success

1035
00:40:28,510 --> 00:40:29,010
rate.

1036
00:40:29,010 --> 00:40:32,390
And I think we could
apply some fancy scaling

1037
00:40:32,390 --> 00:40:34,070
law, trying to curve fitting.

1038
00:40:34,070 --> 00:40:36,290
But we didn't look
too much into that

1039
00:40:36,290 --> 00:40:38,900
because this is a
trend that we expected.

1040
00:40:38,900 --> 00:40:40,910
We just weren't sure
about the magnitude

1041
00:40:40,910 --> 00:40:43,140
of how much it would affect us.

1042
00:40:43,140 --> 00:40:47,120
And I think I don't have
any really good insights

1043
00:40:47,120 --> 00:40:50,306
on this besides that we see
this phenomenon empirically.

1044
00:40:50,306 --> 00:40:50,806
Yeah.

1045
00:40:50,806 --> 00:40:52,590
[INAUDIBLE]

1046
00:40:52,590 --> 00:40:55,790
It would seem that [INAUDIBLE]
there should be less

1047
00:40:55,790 --> 00:41:06,830
[INAUDIBLE] make this
100% or [INAUDIBLE]??

1048
00:41:06,830 --> 00:41:07,580
Great question.

1049
00:41:07,580 --> 00:41:09,050
So the question
is, Oh, maybe this

1050
00:41:09,050 --> 00:41:11,175
will just go on indefinitely,
or is there something

1051
00:41:11,175 --> 00:41:13,100
magical about January 2023?

1052
00:41:13,100 --> 00:41:16,160
But I think this is maybe also--

1053
00:41:16,160 --> 00:41:18,440
This is when we
start to conflate

1054
00:41:18,440 --> 00:41:21,380
the algorithmic exploration with
the practical considerations

1055
00:41:21,380 --> 00:41:23,990
of scaling real-world
operations, which was,

1056
00:41:23,990 --> 00:41:26,150
we got enough data, our
policies were hitting,

1057
00:41:26,150 --> 00:41:28,440
saturating on these
hitting close to 100%.

1058
00:41:28,440 --> 00:41:30,810
We're like, all right, let's
collect another data set.

1059
00:41:30,810 --> 00:41:33,200
So we basically collect
until it's at 100

1060
00:41:33,200 --> 00:41:34,980
and then we switch
to something else.

1061
00:41:34,980 --> 00:41:36,740
But at this point
what was interesting

1062
00:41:36,740 --> 00:41:40,130
is that we bet really big
on this RT-1 architecture.

1063
00:41:40,130 --> 00:41:42,110
We'd already been collecting
demos for a while.

1064
00:41:42,110 --> 00:41:45,090
So it was possible that we had
collected more than we needed.

1065
00:41:45,090 --> 00:41:47,480
And in some cases, actually,
you could cut tasks

1066
00:41:47,480 --> 00:41:49,280
without losing too
much performance, which

1067
00:41:49,280 --> 00:41:50,450
was quite interesting.

1068
00:41:50,450 --> 00:41:53,192
[INAUDIBLE]

1069
00:41:53,192 --> 00:41:59,855
It says it needs
[INAUDIBLE] data

1070
00:41:59,855 --> 00:42:04,913
that might be more diverse among
the different tasks [INAUDIBLE]

1071
00:42:04,913 --> 00:42:05,630
the same task?

1072
00:42:05,630 --> 00:42:07,050
Yeah.

1073
00:42:07,050 --> 00:42:10,490
If you just use the same
object for all the tasks

1074
00:42:10,490 --> 00:42:12,692
or the same [INAUDIBLE].

1075
00:42:12,692 --> 00:42:18,500

1076
00:42:18,500 --> 00:42:19,200
Great question.

1077
00:42:19,200 --> 00:42:20,090
And the question
is whether or not

1078
00:42:20,090 --> 00:42:22,580
all tasks are created equal
in terms of their capacity

1079
00:42:22,580 --> 00:42:24,260
and entropy for
different behaviors you

1080
00:42:24,260 --> 00:42:24,950
can learn from them.

1081
00:42:24,950 --> 00:42:26,325
And yeah, that's
definitely true.

1082
00:42:26,325 --> 00:42:27,648
Some tasks are much easier.

1083
00:42:27,648 --> 00:42:29,690
We have a task that's just
"pick up this object."

1084
00:42:29,690 --> 00:42:31,910
That's going to have much
less interesting stuff

1085
00:42:31,910 --> 00:42:34,520
you can squeeze out of it
than removing something

1086
00:42:34,520 --> 00:42:36,408
into a drawer and then
closing the drawer.

1087
00:42:36,408 --> 00:42:37,700
That's, yeah, a great question.

1088
00:42:37,700 --> 00:42:40,540

1089
00:42:40,540 --> 00:42:41,040
Great.

1090
00:42:41,040 --> 00:42:44,800
Now, ablations, we also trained
without the big model size.

1091
00:42:44,800 --> 00:42:46,840
We did it without
pre-training, without--

1092
00:42:46,840 --> 00:42:48,840
with continuous instead
of discrete actions,

1093
00:42:48,840 --> 00:42:51,870
with autoregressive
actions, without history,

1094
00:42:51,870 --> 00:42:53,490
without the transformer.

1095
00:42:53,490 --> 00:42:55,770
And I think all of
these design choices

1096
00:42:55,770 --> 00:43:00,414
did seem to be required
for robust performance.

1097
00:43:00,414 --> 00:43:02,127
[INAUDIBLE]

1098
00:43:02,127 --> 00:43:02,960
Oh, yeah, of course.

1099
00:43:02,960 --> 00:43:03,956
[INAUDIBLE]

1100
00:43:03,956 --> 00:43:10,430

1101
00:43:10,430 --> 00:43:12,930
[INAUDIBLE]

1102
00:43:12,930 --> 00:43:15,420
Yeah, I think all the--

1103
00:43:15,420 --> 00:43:17,910
and, again, for
paper writing, it's

1104
00:43:17,910 --> 00:43:20,400
the best thing that we
can empirically find.

1105
00:43:20,400 --> 00:43:21,480
That's the method.

1106
00:43:21,480 --> 00:43:23,938
And then, we'll figure out why
each of these are important.

1107
00:43:23,938 --> 00:43:24,660
And so, yeah.

1108
00:43:24,660 --> 00:43:26,850
I think one surprising
thing here, perhaps,

1109
00:43:26,850 --> 00:43:28,540
was that autoregressive
actions hurt.

1110
00:43:28,540 --> 00:43:30,540
You might think that
passing in more information

1111
00:43:30,540 --> 00:43:33,540
is always better than
passing in fewer information.

1112
00:43:33,540 --> 00:43:35,760
But in this case,
maybe conditioning

1113
00:43:35,760 --> 00:43:39,630
on your previous actions was
doing in-context learning.

1114
00:43:39,630 --> 00:43:42,000
It was doing online
systems identification

1115
00:43:42,000 --> 00:43:46,080
to figure out what teleoperator
this data came from

1116
00:43:46,080 --> 00:43:49,680
and how you can overfit that
specific set of action history.

1117
00:43:49,680 --> 00:43:52,860
And so removing that
was actually better,

1118
00:43:52,860 --> 00:43:56,590
one interesting tidbit there.

1119
00:43:56,590 --> 00:43:57,580
Cool then.

1120
00:43:57,580 --> 00:44:00,140
And maybe in the
interest of time,

1121
00:44:00,140 --> 00:44:03,250
I'll try to get through the
other ones a bit more quicker.

1122
00:44:03,250 --> 00:44:04,800
And then we can
maybe just do a few,

1123
00:44:04,800 --> 00:44:06,850
or I'll just do the
questions at the end

1124
00:44:06,850 --> 00:44:08,500
if that's possible,
just so we have

1125
00:44:08,500 --> 00:44:10,605
time to get through everything.

1126
00:44:10,605 --> 00:44:13,230
The next work here, moving a bit
away from skill learning then,

1127
00:44:13,230 --> 00:44:15,000
and actually on to
the planning level,

1128
00:44:15,000 --> 00:44:17,160
I think the first project
took a lot of the design

1129
00:44:17,160 --> 00:44:20,130
principles of other fields
and all this offline robot

1130
00:44:20,130 --> 00:44:22,947
learning paradigm and put
it into the skill learning.

1131
00:44:22,947 --> 00:44:24,780
So it actually bring
that out to other parts

1132
00:44:24,780 --> 00:44:25,960
of the robotic system.

1133
00:44:25,960 --> 00:44:27,660
And the first work
here is SayCan.

1134
00:44:27,660 --> 00:44:30,633
If you remember here, back
in this timeline, in 2022,

1135
00:44:30,633 --> 00:44:32,550
we started thinking
about, Oh, yeah, how do we

1136
00:44:32,550 --> 00:44:37,200
scale this multi-task imitation
learning, but at the same time,

1137
00:44:37,200 --> 00:44:40,320
large language models and other
types of foundation models

1138
00:44:40,320 --> 00:44:42,030
are really picking
up steam, whether it

1139
00:44:42,030 --> 00:44:44,077
was imaging or DALL-E 2.

1140
00:44:44,077 --> 00:44:45,660
And we definitely
wanted to figure out

1141
00:44:45,660 --> 00:44:47,410
how we could use those as well.

1142
00:44:47,410 --> 00:44:49,557
We had come up with
this [INAUDIBLE] design

1143
00:44:49,557 --> 00:44:50,640
that we're betting big on.

1144
00:44:50,640 --> 00:44:53,430
But from here, we
started to explore how

1145
00:44:53,430 --> 00:44:55,470
all of the bitter lessons 2.0--

1146
00:44:55,470 --> 00:44:57,390
we could start utilizing
foundation models

1147
00:44:57,390 --> 00:45:01,600
within the context of
our full stack system.

1148
00:45:01,600 --> 00:45:03,570
The problem of
doing this naively

1149
00:45:03,570 --> 00:45:05,580
is that language models
are not completely

1150
00:45:05,580 --> 00:45:07,440
a very natural fit for robotics.

1151
00:45:07,440 --> 00:45:09,340
For example, if you're
a robot in a kitchen,

1152
00:45:09,340 --> 00:45:11,340
you ask the language
model, "I spilled my drink.

1153
00:45:11,340 --> 00:45:12,110
What can you do?"

1154
00:45:12,110 --> 00:45:14,610
Language model will give you
stuff that's not very relevant.

1155
00:45:14,610 --> 00:45:15,990
It's going to ask
you to vacuum it,

1156
00:45:15,990 --> 00:45:17,730
it's going to ask you
to call a cleaner,

1157
00:45:17,730 --> 00:45:18,925
or it's going to apologize.

1158
00:45:18,925 --> 00:45:20,550
And these are not
things that the robot

1159
00:45:20,550 --> 00:45:24,290
can do in your kitchen with
your spilled drink to help you.

1160
00:45:24,290 --> 00:45:26,830
And so there are two
parts of this then.

1161
00:45:26,830 --> 00:45:29,500
The one issue is that
our robots are limited.

1162
00:45:29,500 --> 00:45:32,267
They are very constrained
with what they can do.

1163
00:45:32,267 --> 00:45:33,350
They cannot do everything.

1164
00:45:33,350 --> 00:45:34,900
But they can do certain things.

1165
00:45:34,900 --> 00:45:38,980
And then the second problem
is that the language models

1166
00:45:38,980 --> 00:45:39,980
are also constrained.

1167
00:45:39,980 --> 00:45:41,480
They don't know
what the robot sees.

1168
00:45:41,480 --> 00:45:43,630
They don't understand that
they are in a robot body

1169
00:45:43,630 --> 00:45:46,210
in a micro kitchen,
needing to do real stuff

1170
00:45:46,210 --> 00:45:47,570
in the physical world.

1171
00:45:47,570 --> 00:45:51,130
And so, we need to get the
robots to speak language model

1172
00:45:51,130 --> 00:45:54,970
language and then the language
model to speak robot language.

1173
00:45:54,970 --> 00:45:57,730
To do this, we present SayCan.

1174
00:45:57,730 --> 00:46:00,520
In the same setting, "please
put an apple on the table."

1175
00:46:00,520 --> 00:46:03,460
We score the predictions
of the language model

1176
00:46:03,460 --> 00:46:05,530
on a constrained
set of tasks that we

1177
00:46:05,530 --> 00:46:07,783
know the robot has
been trained to do.

1178
00:46:07,783 --> 00:46:09,700
And then, we also take
the affordance function

1179
00:46:09,700 --> 00:46:10,325
from the robot.

1180
00:46:10,325 --> 00:46:13,300
And the affordance function
is an estimation of,

1181
00:46:13,300 --> 00:46:16,900
given some kind of state,
what the robot is able to do,

1182
00:46:16,900 --> 00:46:19,240
how confident it is that it
can successfully accomplish

1183
00:46:19,240 --> 00:46:20,625
that task in the given state.

1184
00:46:20,625 --> 00:46:22,750
In our case, we use something
like a value function

1185
00:46:22,750 --> 00:46:25,780
for reinforcement learning
which accomplishes this quality.

1186
00:46:25,780 --> 00:46:27,757
Given these two values,
these two scores,

1187
00:46:27,757 --> 00:46:29,590
we have the confidence
from a language model

1188
00:46:29,590 --> 00:46:31,820
and then the confidence
from the robot.

1189
00:46:31,820 --> 00:46:32,800
We can combine these.

1190
00:46:32,800 --> 00:46:34,780
And then, hopefully,
the combined prediction

1191
00:46:34,780 --> 00:46:37,090
is both something that's
going to be very semantically

1192
00:46:37,090 --> 00:46:38,980
relevant for the
high-level instruction.

1193
00:46:38,980 --> 00:46:40,630
Finding an apple
is the first step

1194
00:46:40,630 --> 00:46:42,373
and, "please put an
apple on the table."

1195
00:46:42,373 --> 00:46:44,290
But it's also something
that the robot can do.

1196
00:46:44,290 --> 00:46:45,832
There's no robot in
the frame, but it

1197
00:46:45,832 --> 00:46:47,980
knows that it's been
trained to find an apple.

1198
00:46:47,980 --> 00:46:49,640
So it can navigate
around to find it.

1199
00:46:49,640 --> 00:46:51,932
And so, hopefully, we can do
this then in a closed loop

1200
00:46:51,932 --> 00:46:53,830
and then keep on
going and predicting

1201
00:46:53,830 --> 00:46:55,570
a high-level plan from
the language model

1202
00:46:55,570 --> 00:46:57,940
that's grounded with the
affordance function of what

1203
00:46:57,940 --> 00:47:01,410
the robot understands.

1204
00:47:01,410 --> 00:47:04,320
There's a video here of the
SayCan doing different stuff,

1205
00:47:04,320 --> 00:47:07,260
but happy to share
it later offline.

1206
00:47:07,260 --> 00:47:08,618
It's very cool, trust me.

1207
00:47:08,618 --> 00:47:10,410
It's the greatest thing
since sliced bread.

1208
00:47:10,410 --> 00:47:16,010

1209
00:47:16,010 --> 00:47:18,170
And, yeah, some numbers, then.

1210
00:47:18,170 --> 00:47:21,470
We tested this out on very
long-horizon instructions,

1211
00:47:21,470 --> 00:47:24,230
encompassing more than
10 separate navigation

1212
00:47:24,230 --> 00:47:26,660
and manipulation skills
in the micro kitchen

1213
00:47:26,660 --> 00:47:29,030
that you see on
the bottom right.

1214
00:47:29,030 --> 00:47:33,050
We evaluated hundreds of
different evaluations on this.

1215
00:47:33,050 --> 00:47:35,880
And we tested out very-- a
lot of different concepts,

1216
00:47:35,880 --> 00:47:38,810
including things like rephrasing
by using single primitives

1217
00:47:38,810 --> 00:47:42,680
by drawing instructions that
just came from colleagues

1218
00:47:42,680 --> 00:47:43,790
and friends.

1219
00:47:43,790 --> 00:47:47,030
And then we found that
while they were failures

1220
00:47:47,030 --> 00:47:49,490
in both the language model
planning stuff side, where

1221
00:47:49,490 --> 00:47:52,190
it would predict the wrong
path for the current situation,

1222
00:47:52,190 --> 00:47:54,770
as well as on a policy execution
side, even when it gets

1223
00:47:54,770 --> 00:47:56,900
a good plan, the robot
will mess up sometimes,

1224
00:47:56,900 --> 00:48:01,410
overall, it was still
doing quite well.

1225
00:48:01,410 --> 00:48:05,030
And now, let's kick
this back to the lesson.

1226
00:48:05,030 --> 00:48:07,940
I think this is a very
great example of how

1227
00:48:07,940 --> 00:48:11,120
we can leverage internet
scale foundation

1228
00:48:11,120 --> 00:48:12,268
models as they get better.

1229
00:48:12,268 --> 00:48:14,810
When we started the project, we
started with a language model

1230
00:48:14,810 --> 00:48:16,670
called FLAN from Google.

1231
00:48:16,670 --> 00:48:19,100
Throughout our
implementation, our PaLM

1232
00:48:19,100 --> 00:48:20,810
came online, Pathways
Language Model.

1233
00:48:20,810 --> 00:48:24,080
And when that happened, we were
able to just hot-swap it in.

1234
00:48:24,080 --> 00:48:27,020
And performance just
got better for free

1235
00:48:27,020 --> 00:48:28,430
without us having
to do anything.

1236
00:48:28,430 --> 00:48:31,100
By just assuming that
language was the API,

1237
00:48:31,100 --> 00:48:32,843
the plan just has
to be any string.

1238
00:48:32,843 --> 00:48:34,010
It can come from any source.

1239
00:48:34,010 --> 00:48:34,880
It can come from a human.

1240
00:48:34,880 --> 00:48:36,297
It can come from
a language model.

1241
00:48:36,297 --> 00:48:37,940
When we improve
that language model,

1242
00:48:37,940 --> 00:48:39,890
the system gets better overall.

1243
00:48:39,890 --> 00:48:42,020
And here you see,
with a scaling size,

1244
00:48:42,020 --> 00:48:44,360
as the model LLM
increased in size,

1245
00:48:44,360 --> 00:48:46,370
our planning performance
got even better.

1246
00:48:46,370 --> 00:48:50,440

1247
00:48:50,440 --> 00:48:53,530
And some cool tricks here
to get it working, well,

1248
00:48:53,530 --> 00:48:55,220
how do we actually
produce this plan?

1249
00:48:55,220 --> 00:48:58,390
Well, while just by prompting
as is the rage these days,

1250
00:48:58,390 --> 00:49:00,850
with chain-of-thought and
with better prompting, of just

1251
00:49:00,850 --> 00:49:03,940
giving it examples of, here
are some great robot plans,

1252
00:49:03,940 --> 00:49:06,220
now give me a new
plan starting with

1253
00:49:06,220 --> 00:49:07,620
this high-level instruction.

1254
00:49:07,620 --> 00:49:09,370
We saw that the robot
could do all things,

1255
00:49:09,370 --> 00:49:13,900
from understanding different
languages to asking them

1256
00:49:13,900 --> 00:49:15,577
to do very complex
reasoning, like hey,

1257
00:49:15,577 --> 00:49:18,160
give me something caffeinated
or "I don't do caffeine anymore.

1258
00:49:18,160 --> 00:49:20,050
Get me something better."

1259
00:49:20,050 --> 00:49:22,480
Or bring me a healthy
snack versus bring me

1260
00:49:22,480 --> 00:49:23,530
an unhealthy snack.

1261
00:49:23,530 --> 00:49:28,110
So they can was able to
reason through all of these.

1262
00:49:28,110 --> 00:49:32,600
But I think that was our kind
of the first contact of robotics

1263
00:49:32,600 --> 00:49:34,260
with language
models on our team.

1264
00:49:34,260 --> 00:49:36,350
And it was the first
exploration into how

1265
00:49:36,350 --> 00:49:38,088
these two worlds could overlap.

1266
00:49:38,088 --> 00:49:40,130
There were definitely
still improvements, though.

1267
00:49:40,130 --> 00:49:42,500
And in Inner Monologue, we
try to improve those further

1268
00:49:42,500 --> 00:49:45,400
by bringing in vision
language models.

1269
00:49:45,400 --> 00:49:49,450
The idea here is that we
had a very high planned rate

1270
00:49:49,450 --> 00:49:52,840
success with SayCan,
but unfortunately, it

1271
00:49:52,840 --> 00:49:54,940
wasn't really able to
recover from failures.

1272
00:49:54,940 --> 00:49:57,820
What I mean by that is that the
language model would not really

1273
00:49:57,820 --> 00:49:59,930
get updates of what was
going on in the world.

1274
00:49:59,930 --> 00:50:02,440
So that if this was the plan
it proposed, go to the table,

1275
00:50:02,440 --> 00:50:04,900
pick up a coke, bring it
to you, but you messed up

1276
00:50:04,900 --> 00:50:06,950
picking the coke can,
you drop it on the floor,

1277
00:50:06,950 --> 00:50:09,033
it would still continue
trying to bring it to you.

1278
00:50:09,033 --> 00:50:09,580
Put it aside.

1279
00:50:09,580 --> 00:50:11,140
But all of that does not
really matter anymore

1280
00:50:11,140 --> 00:50:12,650
because you drop the coke can.

1281
00:50:12,650 --> 00:50:15,790
And so, in this work,
Inner Monologue,

1282
00:50:15,790 --> 00:50:17,470
we are really
hoping to figure out

1283
00:50:17,470 --> 00:50:20,260
how we could add
closed-loop dynamic feedback

1284
00:50:20,260 --> 00:50:24,150
from the environment into
this planning process.

1285
00:50:24,150 --> 00:50:26,490
Let's take that
exact same example.

1286
00:50:26,490 --> 00:50:29,730
Now, instead of just directly
correcting every instruction,

1287
00:50:29,730 --> 00:50:31,860
maybe we add back some
feedback from the scene

1288
00:50:31,860 --> 00:50:35,650
also conveyed using language
as the universal API here.

1289
00:50:35,650 --> 00:50:37,650
The scene can tell you
what's actually in there.

1290
00:50:37,650 --> 00:50:40,445
Maybe the robot asks a
question now in the robot.

1291
00:50:40,445 --> 00:50:42,570
This is the language model
asking the clarification

1292
00:50:42,570 --> 00:50:43,200
question.

1293
00:50:43,200 --> 00:50:46,110
Maybe hear a human response
or another language model,

1294
00:50:46,110 --> 00:50:48,600
then you can predict the
action or the next task

1295
00:50:48,600 --> 00:50:51,060
to do once the language
model has enough context.

1296
00:50:51,060 --> 00:50:54,210
And maybe you even add in
stuff like success detection,

1297
00:50:54,210 --> 00:50:55,930
and so on and so forth.

1298
00:50:55,930 --> 00:50:57,270
How do we do this, then?

1299
00:50:57,270 --> 00:51:00,120
Well, the first thing
that we implement

1300
00:51:00,120 --> 00:51:02,580
is what we call passive
scene description.

1301
00:51:02,580 --> 00:51:05,250
Just using either an
off-the-shelf engineer

1302
00:51:05,250 --> 00:51:07,380
heuristic, using object
detection models,

1303
00:51:07,380 --> 00:51:10,920
something like ViLD, you can
describe the scene in text

1304
00:51:10,920 --> 00:51:15,220
and just convey all of that
context to the language model.

1305
00:51:15,220 --> 00:51:16,750
For active scene
description, this

1306
00:51:16,750 --> 00:51:18,670
is maybe similar to
visual question answering

1307
00:51:18,670 --> 00:51:21,010
if you're familiar
with that field.

1308
00:51:21,010 --> 00:51:23,980
The language model can
actually propose active queries

1309
00:51:23,980 --> 00:51:25,960
that it's curious
about in the scene,

1310
00:51:25,960 --> 00:51:28,780
maybe to make sure that it
has enough context to move on.

1311
00:51:28,780 --> 00:51:31,120
And here, either a human
can provide the answer,

1312
00:51:31,120 --> 00:51:34,120
or in the future, a VQA
model, as they improve,

1313
00:51:34,120 --> 00:51:36,390
can provide that.

1314
00:51:36,390 --> 00:51:38,120
And finally, for
success detection,

1315
00:51:38,120 --> 00:51:41,060
this is very important to allow
the language model planner

1316
00:51:41,060 --> 00:51:43,790
to know when to try
to retry something.

1317
00:51:43,790 --> 00:51:46,700
Here, we take in the first
and last image, fine-tune

1318
00:51:46,700 --> 00:51:48,500
a [INAUDIBLE] success
detector, and use

1319
00:51:48,500 --> 00:51:51,680
that to provide binary
success/failure information

1320
00:51:51,680 --> 00:51:52,820
back to our language model.

1321
00:51:52,820 --> 00:51:56,300

1322
00:51:56,300 --> 00:51:57,320
And for the--

1323
00:51:57,320 --> 00:52:01,310
Results wise, we can see a very
similar SayCan long-horizon

1324
00:52:01,310 --> 00:52:03,380
evaluation, but
here we actually--

1325
00:52:03,380 --> 00:52:06,020
what's interesting is
that we're able to--

1326
00:52:06,020 --> 00:52:09,260
and basically implement all
of these different automated

1327
00:52:09,260 --> 00:52:11,750
feedback mechanisms
on the robot, and so

1328
00:52:11,750 --> 00:52:14,390
that it's able to reason
and recover from things.

1329
00:52:14,390 --> 00:52:18,950
Here, you see, it's going
to try to go to the table.

1330
00:52:18,950 --> 00:52:21,210
But the humans actually
have been saying, hey,

1331
00:52:21,210 --> 00:52:22,280
I changed my mind.

1332
00:52:22,280 --> 00:52:24,530
And then it changes-- the
human change its mind again,

1333
00:52:24,530 --> 00:52:25,830
asking it to go back and forth.

1334
00:52:25,830 --> 00:52:28,527
And the robot's able to-- maybe
we're torturing the language

1335
00:52:28,527 --> 00:52:29,360
model at this point.

1336
00:52:29,360 --> 00:52:32,360
But the language is able
to replan and make sure

1337
00:52:32,360 --> 00:52:34,130
that the human
intent is satisfied.

1338
00:52:34,130 --> 00:52:38,310

1339
00:52:38,310 --> 00:52:39,180
We also tried.

1340
00:52:39,180 --> 00:52:40,950
I'm not sure if
this video shows it.

1341
00:52:40,950 --> 00:52:43,770
But situations where we did
adversarial inputs where I

1342
00:52:43,770 --> 00:52:45,422
walked around and just--

1343
00:52:45,422 --> 00:52:47,130
knocking objects out
of the robot's hands

1344
00:52:47,130 --> 00:52:50,785
and forcing the success detector
to tell it, hey, you messed up.

1345
00:52:50,785 --> 00:52:51,285
Try again.

1346
00:52:51,285 --> 00:52:57,130

1347
00:52:57,130 --> 00:52:59,350
And we also tried
this out on a couple

1348
00:52:59,350 --> 00:53:01,900
of different domains, a
simulated tabletop manipulation

1349
00:53:01,900 --> 00:53:04,360
domain as well as a real-world
manipulation domain.

1350
00:53:04,360 --> 00:53:06,730
And we found that
this was much better

1351
00:53:06,730 --> 00:53:09,910
than SayCan or, let's
say, just only using

1352
00:53:09,910 --> 00:53:13,930
visual features themselves,
with something like CLIPort.

1353
00:53:13,930 --> 00:53:18,340
And I think here, it really
speaks towards a trend

1354
00:53:18,340 --> 00:53:20,320
that I really come
to appreciate.

1355
00:53:20,320 --> 00:53:22,295
In 2018, a robotics
professor once

1356
00:53:22,295 --> 00:53:24,670
said that when they looked at
all of the different things

1357
00:53:24,670 --> 00:53:27,070
preventing robot learning
from scaling tremendously,

1358
00:53:27,070 --> 00:53:29,612
they thought the bottleneck was
high-level semantic planning,

1359
00:53:29,612 --> 00:53:31,130
about reasoning,
about common sense.

1360
00:53:31,130 --> 00:53:34,690
And I think in 2022 and
2023, language models

1361
00:53:34,690 --> 00:53:39,400
can provide one path of
how this can be offloaded,

1362
00:53:39,400 --> 00:53:40,570
at least in the interim.

1363
00:53:40,570 --> 00:53:43,720
And I think if language
models are the API,

1364
00:53:43,720 --> 00:53:46,510
then you can just bring in
these vision language models.

1365
00:53:46,510 --> 00:53:49,090
As object detectors get
better, as success detectors,

1366
00:53:49,090 --> 00:53:50,800
as VQA, as language
models get better,

1367
00:53:50,800 --> 00:53:52,600
you can bring them
all into the fold.

1368
00:53:52,600 --> 00:53:54,430
And the act is a life vest.

1369
00:53:54,430 --> 00:53:58,090
If your robot currently does
not have common sense reasoning,

1370
00:53:58,090 --> 00:54:00,730
these other models can act
as a scaffold in a life vest

1371
00:54:00,730 --> 00:54:03,160
to bring you up to par with
what they currently love.

1372
00:54:03,160 --> 00:54:04,810
And maybe then, in
the future, you'll

1373
00:54:04,810 --> 00:54:06,652
get beyond what the
language models know.

1374
00:54:06,652 --> 00:54:08,110
But in the short
term, it does seem

1375
00:54:08,110 --> 00:54:09,943
that we can leverage
them to accelerate what

1376
00:54:09,943 --> 00:54:13,200
we can do in the real world.

1377
00:54:13,200 --> 00:54:14,430
Moving on now from--

1378
00:54:14,430 --> 00:54:17,040
we saw now how language
models could do planning.

1379
00:54:17,040 --> 00:54:19,490
We saw how vision language
models could help planning.

1380
00:54:19,490 --> 00:54:21,240
And now, we're going
to switch gears a bit

1381
00:54:21,240 --> 00:54:23,220
and think about how
vision language models can

1382
00:54:23,220 --> 00:54:25,200
help other aspects
of the bottlenecks

1383
00:54:25,200 --> 00:54:27,720
that robot learning faces.

1384
00:54:27,720 --> 00:54:32,100
One of these is that data
collection is very expensive.

1385
00:54:32,100 --> 00:54:36,180
As we mentioned before, we
did have this 130,000-episode

1386
00:54:36,180 --> 00:54:39,600
demonstration data set, but
it was collected over a year

1387
00:54:39,600 --> 00:54:44,010
and a half at significant cost,
both in resources and time,

1388
00:54:44,010 --> 00:54:47,460
and money and with
many, many robots.

1389
00:54:47,460 --> 00:54:52,020
And, of course, these tasks,
too, we're also a bit limited.

1390
00:54:52,020 --> 00:54:54,510
We use 700
very-templated commands,

1391
00:54:54,510 --> 00:54:57,180
instructions that we would give
to teleoperators because we

1392
00:54:57,180 --> 00:54:59,490
knew that this would scale.

1393
00:54:59,490 --> 00:55:02,550
If we collected enough data for
each of these templated tasks,

1394
00:55:02,550 --> 00:55:05,023
we could do that specific task.

1395
00:55:05,023 --> 00:55:07,440
And here's the flow that someone
was asking about earlier.

1396
00:55:07,440 --> 00:55:09,360
We give this "pick
coke can" instruction.

1397
00:55:09,360 --> 00:55:11,580
The operator controls the
robot in the real world,

1398
00:55:11,580 --> 00:55:14,310
finished the task, marked
steps that it has terminated,

1399
00:55:14,310 --> 00:55:17,220
and then, stage that out to
this big orange data set.

1400
00:55:17,220 --> 00:55:18,720
And that big orange
data set is what

1401
00:55:18,720 --> 00:55:20,940
we trained on and all
of the previous projects

1402
00:55:20,940 --> 00:55:22,860
for the control policies.

1403
00:55:22,860 --> 00:55:24,450
What we additionally
considered was

1404
00:55:24,450 --> 00:55:27,300
adding a bit of crowdsourced
hindsight annotation.

1405
00:55:27,300 --> 00:55:30,270
If you're familiar with
Hindsight Experience Replay

1406
00:55:30,270 --> 00:55:33,360
in reinforcement learning
with goal-conditioning with--

1407
00:55:33,360 --> 00:55:35,850
maybe the robot did
something that wasn't just

1408
00:55:35,850 --> 00:55:37,680
this high-level
template instruction.

1409
00:55:37,680 --> 00:55:41,070
We could ask a human to
describe more verbosely what

1410
00:55:41,070 --> 00:55:41,700
the robot did.

1411
00:55:41,700 --> 00:55:43,200
Maybe it picked up
the coke can that was

1412
00:55:43,200 --> 00:55:44,320
on the right side of the table.

1413
00:55:44,320 --> 00:55:46,350
Maybe it picked it up
and then knocked it over.

1414
00:55:46,350 --> 00:55:48,720
Maybe it moved it very
slowly to the middle.

1415
00:55:48,720 --> 00:55:51,180
There's a lot of semantic
diversity encompassed

1416
00:55:51,180 --> 00:55:55,590
in this demonstration
that is not totally

1417
00:55:55,590 --> 00:55:57,810
caught by this
high-level templated

1418
00:55:57,810 --> 00:55:59,590
"pick coke can" instruction.

1419
00:55:59,590 --> 00:56:03,420
So we labeled 3% of this big
orange data set with these very

1420
00:56:03,420 --> 00:56:06,360
verbose descriptions.

1421
00:56:06,360 --> 00:56:10,440
And next, we applied the
pseudo-label strategy

1422
00:56:10,440 --> 00:56:12,873
that's been seen in other
fields, such as video

1423
00:56:12,873 --> 00:56:14,790
pretraining with their
inverse dynamics model.

1424
00:56:14,790 --> 00:56:17,190
But instead, we apply
that to the instructions,

1425
00:56:17,190 --> 00:56:20,440
to the semantics of what's
contained in your data set.

1426
00:56:20,440 --> 00:56:23,610
So step one, we be
pre-train a clip model

1427
00:56:23,610 --> 00:56:28,700
on your small labeled data
set of 3% of your main data.

1428
00:56:28,700 --> 00:56:32,960
Then, you go ahead and use that
trained-VLM data to label all

1429
00:56:32,960 --> 00:56:35,900
of the templated instruction
demonstrations that you had

1430
00:56:35,900 --> 00:56:39,300
before that
130,000-episode data set.

1431
00:56:39,300 --> 00:56:40,880
And now you have a
relabeled data set

1432
00:56:40,880 --> 00:56:44,000
which has a large diversity
of interesting semantic

1433
00:56:44,000 --> 00:56:45,650
constructions.

1434
00:56:45,650 --> 00:56:49,550
And then, we plug in all of
these data sets into RT-1

1435
00:56:49,550 --> 00:56:51,230
and just train a
language condition

1436
00:56:51,230 --> 00:56:55,550
behavior cloning policy similar
to how we would normally.

1437
00:56:55,550 --> 00:56:58,400
Even though normally we just use
data set B, the orange one, now

1438
00:56:58,400 --> 00:57:01,460
we use all three data sets.

1439
00:57:01,460 --> 00:57:04,940
And then, finally, we evaluate
on entirely new unseen

1440
00:57:04,940 --> 00:57:06,230
instructions.

1441
00:57:06,230 --> 00:57:10,910
In the prior works, we were
evaluating mainly on these 700

1442
00:57:10,910 --> 00:57:11,955
templated instructions.

1443
00:57:11,955 --> 00:57:13,830
But in this work, we
actually go beyond that.

1444
00:57:13,830 --> 00:57:16,670
We can type in almost
anything you want

1445
00:57:16,670 --> 00:57:17,990
that you think might succeed.

1446
00:57:17,990 --> 00:57:19,730
And you can phrase
it how you can.

1447
00:57:19,730 --> 00:57:20,480
You can add typos.

1448
00:57:20,480 --> 00:57:24,200
You can even do it by
referring to semantic concepts.

1449
00:57:24,200 --> 00:57:25,490
You can add spatial concepts.

1450
00:57:25,490 --> 00:57:27,005
And we see how it does--

1451
00:57:27,005 --> 00:57:29,630

1452
00:57:29,630 --> 00:57:31,520
The reason that this
might work, maybe

1453
00:57:31,520 --> 00:57:33,320
visually to represent
this, is here

1454
00:57:33,320 --> 00:57:36,740
are the [INAUDIBLE] embeddings
on the left and the right.

1455
00:57:36,740 --> 00:57:39,080
It's the same embeddings,
but on the left,

1456
00:57:39,080 --> 00:57:43,250
they're colored by the original
templated instruction that was

1457
00:57:43,250 --> 00:57:45,440
used to collect that episode.

1458
00:57:45,440 --> 00:57:48,410
And on the right is what
the vision language model

1459
00:57:48,410 --> 00:57:52,160
thinks if it's allowed to put
a free-form natural language

1460
00:57:52,160 --> 00:57:54,062
caption and assign
it to that episode.

1461
00:57:54,062 --> 00:57:56,270
You see that on the left,
you have these big clusters

1462
00:57:56,270 --> 00:57:57,380
of "pick coke can."

1463
00:57:57,380 --> 00:57:59,770
It's like hundreds or
thousands of episodes.

1464
00:57:59,770 --> 00:58:01,520
But we all just call
them "pick coke can."

1465
00:58:01,520 --> 00:58:04,100
On the right, then we can then
expand those concepts and say,

1466
00:58:04,100 --> 00:58:07,370
actually, this episode is
picking up the red coke can.

1467
00:58:07,370 --> 00:58:10,190
This episode is picking
up the clumped coke can.

1468
00:58:10,190 --> 00:58:12,860
This is picking up the coke can
that's next to the chip bag.

1469
00:58:12,860 --> 00:58:14,870
And so you can get
a lot more mileage

1470
00:58:14,870 --> 00:58:18,170
out of the same underlying
data set by just using language

1471
00:58:18,170 --> 00:58:20,870
as the diversity mechanism
through which you

1472
00:58:20,870 --> 00:58:22,970
expand the concepts
that you're considering.

1473
00:58:22,970 --> 00:58:24,470
And, for example,
in the middle, you

1474
00:58:24,470 --> 00:58:27,590
see "open top drawer" can
become "hold and pull out

1475
00:58:27,590 --> 00:58:28,400
the top drawer."

1476
00:58:28,400 --> 00:58:30,380
We have stuff like
"the center-left"

1477
00:58:30,380 --> 00:58:32,630
for the middle episode.

1478
00:58:32,630 --> 00:58:34,520
For the bottom one,
"pick green rice chips

1479
00:58:34,520 --> 00:58:37,010
from white bowl" becomes,
"lift up the grape chip bag

1480
00:58:37,010 --> 00:58:38,420
from the bowl bow and
drop it at the bottom

1481
00:58:38,420 --> 00:58:39,530
left corner of the table."

1482
00:58:39,530 --> 00:58:42,890
So you get a lot of these
semantic, spatial concepts that

1483
00:58:42,890 --> 00:58:46,534
are now going to be in your
target supervised labels.

1484
00:58:46,534 --> 00:58:47,713
Yeah, a question.

1485
00:58:47,713 --> 00:58:48,213
Yes.

1486
00:58:48,213 --> 00:58:56,480
So using [INAUDIBLE] this
is expected [INAUDIBLE]..

1487
00:58:56,480 --> 00:58:57,940
You have your [INAUDIBLE].

1488
00:58:57,940 --> 00:59:10,640

1489
00:59:10,640 --> 00:59:12,560
Yeah, great question.

1490
00:59:12,560 --> 00:59:15,200
So I guess, if I
can rephrase a bit,

1491
00:59:15,200 --> 00:59:17,180
the problem is
that it's actually

1492
00:59:17,180 --> 00:59:19,730
a very difficult and
perhaps even intractable

1493
00:59:19,730 --> 00:59:22,430
problem of how you map all the
linguistic concepts you see out

1494
00:59:22,430 --> 00:59:25,130
in the wild down
to maybe embodied

1495
00:59:25,130 --> 00:59:27,110
specific types of episodes.

1496
00:59:27,110 --> 00:59:30,290
And here, maybe I would say
is that we are definitely

1497
00:59:30,290 --> 00:59:33,140
introducing a lot of our
priors and our biases

1498
00:59:33,140 --> 00:59:37,130
onto maybe what we call as left,
you mean left 10 centimeters,

1499
00:59:37,130 --> 00:59:39,920
left two centimeters.

1500
00:59:39,920 --> 00:59:41,120
What do words mean?

1501
00:59:41,120 --> 00:59:42,590
And these definitions,
what do they

1502
00:59:42,590 --> 00:59:44,870
mean to us, to the
crowd compute raters

1503
00:59:44,870 --> 00:59:46,160
that generated these captions?

1504
00:59:46,160 --> 00:59:47,270
What do they mean to the robot?

1505
00:59:47,270 --> 00:59:48,620
What do they mean to
the language models?

1506
00:59:48,620 --> 00:59:50,245
Maybe these are all
slightly different.

1507
00:59:50,245 --> 00:59:54,020
But the hope is at least
if they're roughly similar.

1508
00:59:54,020 --> 00:59:57,140
We'll get directionally
correct improvements.

1509
00:59:57,140 --> 01:00:00,650
So I would say the nuances
of these specific hard lines

1510
01:00:00,650 --> 01:00:04,520
of definitions and
actual semantic meaning

1511
01:00:04,520 --> 01:00:08,390
of these words, I think that's
maybe out of scope right now,

1512
01:00:08,390 --> 01:00:10,400
but maybe something
we'll dive into further.

1513
01:00:10,400 --> 01:00:11,870
At a higher level,
though, I think,

1514
01:00:11,870 --> 01:00:13,430
basically, the bar
is just so low.

1515
01:00:13,430 --> 01:00:15,770
We have these 700
template instructions

1516
01:00:15,770 --> 01:00:17,750
that are basically one-hot IDs.

1517
01:00:17,750 --> 01:00:21,060
And we just want to make those
closer to natural language,

1518
01:00:21,060 --> 01:00:22,520
even if by a little.

1519
01:00:22,520 --> 01:00:25,310
And I think, at
least, we're trying

1520
01:00:25,310 --> 01:00:27,890
to get towards that with these
visual language models that

1521
01:00:27,890 --> 01:00:29,840
are captioning automatically.

1522
01:00:29,840 --> 01:00:33,110
I hope that answers
your question.

1523
01:00:33,110 --> 01:00:36,620
And we also compare
it to a few baselines.

1524
01:00:36,620 --> 01:00:38,900
On the top left,
here, we look at what

1525
01:00:38,900 --> 01:00:43,250
if we only train on this 3% of
these fancy human-rated labels.

1526
01:00:43,250 --> 01:00:46,440
What if we only train on
the original RT-1 data sets?

1527
01:00:46,440 --> 01:00:48,170
What if we train
on both of these?

1528
01:00:48,170 --> 01:00:50,090
And what if we train
on both of these,

1529
01:00:50,090 --> 01:00:53,360
plus all of the predictions
given by our VLM?

1530
01:00:53,360 --> 01:00:57,230
And what's interesting
here is that relabeling

1531
01:00:57,230 --> 01:00:58,910
seems to universally help.

1532
01:00:58,910 --> 01:01:01,920
We evaluated only on
novel instructions

1533
01:01:01,920 --> 01:01:03,170
that was new for this project.

1534
01:01:03,170 --> 01:01:05,540
It's the first time on a
robotics project where we only

1535
01:01:05,540 --> 01:01:06,697
tested it on something--

1536
01:01:06,697 --> 01:01:08,030
I could type whatever I thought.

1537
01:01:08,030 --> 01:01:10,430
I'd type it in, and that
became the test set.

1538
01:01:10,430 --> 01:01:12,860
And we just have to make sure
that it was never contained

1539
01:01:12,860 --> 01:01:15,310
in the training coverage.

1540
01:01:15,310 --> 01:01:17,060
And you see all these
interesting examples

1541
01:01:17,060 --> 01:01:19,820
on the right here of stuff
like "move the lonely object

1542
01:01:19,820 --> 01:01:20,450
to the others."

1543
01:01:20,450 --> 01:01:22,910
I have no idea how this worked.

1544
01:01:22,910 --> 01:01:25,400
Stuff like lifting
the yellow rectangle,

1545
01:01:25,400 --> 01:01:27,320
talking about colors,
talking about "move

1546
01:01:27,320 --> 01:01:28,733
the right apple to the left."

1547
01:01:28,733 --> 01:01:30,650
Here, we actually had
two apples in the scene.

1548
01:01:30,650 --> 01:01:33,380
And actually, in our
training demonstration data,

1549
01:01:33,380 --> 01:01:36,428
we never collected scenes
with duplicate objects.

1550
01:01:36,428 --> 01:01:38,720
Just because we thought of
this multi-modality problem,

1551
01:01:38,720 --> 01:01:40,910
if you just say, "pick coke
cans and these two coke cans."

1552
01:01:40,910 --> 01:01:43,493
It's going to be very difficult
to figure out which one to do.

1553
01:01:43,493 --> 01:01:45,710
But with language labeling,
it seems like maybe we

1554
01:01:45,710 --> 01:01:46,470
could do that now.

1555
01:01:46,470 --> 01:01:48,887
So even though we never trained
on scenes with two apples,

1556
01:01:48,887 --> 01:01:51,740
now you could evaluate on them
and just specify with language

1557
01:01:51,740 --> 01:01:53,120
which apple you want to go for.

1558
01:01:53,120 --> 01:01:56,830
And it was working
pretty reasonably.

1559
01:01:56,830 --> 01:01:58,420
And finally, for
the last example,

1560
01:01:58,420 --> 01:02:00,430
here, I thought it
was interesting,

1561
01:02:00,430 --> 01:02:02,860
a coke can-- we try to
do a novel behavior.

1562
01:02:02,860 --> 01:02:06,520
A push towards the left was
not a templated instruction.

1563
01:02:06,520 --> 01:02:11,677
We only had "move coke can near
Y," where Y is another object,

1564
01:02:11,677 --> 01:02:12,760
"move coke can near apple.

1565
01:02:12,760 --> 01:02:14,350
Move coke can near your sponge."

1566
01:02:14,350 --> 01:02:18,160
So pushing this motion, of
just pushing the coke can

1567
01:02:18,160 --> 01:02:20,140
into the air essentially
was not something

1568
01:02:20,140 --> 01:02:21,190
that we ever encompassed.

1569
01:02:21,190 --> 01:02:22,773
But maybe it was in
one of the labels.

1570
01:02:22,773 --> 01:02:25,678
Maybe if you've seen like
"move coke near apple"

1571
01:02:25,678 --> 01:02:27,220
and the apple is on
the left, and you

1572
01:02:27,220 --> 01:02:30,170
saw "move coke can near sponge,"
and the sponge is on the left,

1573
01:02:30,170 --> 01:02:32,628
you would generate the model,
could generalize and be like,

1574
01:02:32,628 --> 01:02:35,865
oh, left means this side of the
table, not a specific object.

1575
01:02:35,865 --> 01:02:37,240
So maybe that's
what's happening.

1576
01:02:37,240 --> 01:02:38,470
But it's very unclear.

1577
01:02:38,470 --> 01:02:41,650
This is, as I
said, just I type--

1578
01:02:41,650 --> 01:02:44,328
I thought of something, I typed
it, and just saw what happened.

1579
01:02:44,328 --> 01:02:46,120
And we definitely hope
to explore this more

1580
01:02:46,120 --> 01:02:48,220
quantitatively in the future.

1581
01:02:48,220 --> 01:02:49,720
Bottom left, of
course, is, I think,

1582
01:02:49,720 --> 01:02:52,010
comparing against
non-visual augmentation.

1583
01:02:52,010 --> 01:02:54,460
So maybe you can also get
these interesting concepts just

1584
01:02:54,460 --> 01:02:56,140
from language alone.

1585
01:02:56,140 --> 01:02:59,470
Here, we had adding random
noise, or we do Matlab style,

1586
01:02:59,470 --> 01:03:01,330
just swapping out
words, or we even

1587
01:03:01,330 --> 01:03:04,990
use LLM GPT-3, in this
case, to propose rephrasing

1588
01:03:04,990 --> 01:03:06,280
of existing instructions.

1589
01:03:06,280 --> 01:03:09,165
But I think my takeaway
there is that you really

1590
01:03:09,165 --> 01:03:11,290
need visual grounding for
the visual language model

1591
01:03:11,290 --> 01:03:14,440
to say, actually, yeah,
this caption is factually

1592
01:03:14,440 --> 01:03:16,210
accurate at this
given point in time,

1593
01:03:16,210 --> 01:03:19,450
and that it's something
perhaps that would

1594
01:03:19,450 --> 01:03:21,010
be interesting for a robot.

1595
01:03:21,010 --> 01:03:23,019
That fine-tuning process
provides both of those.

1596
01:03:23,019 --> 01:03:23,977
Yeah.

1597
01:03:23,977 --> 01:03:33,100
[INAUDIBLE]

1598
01:03:33,100 --> 01:03:34,720
Yeah, definitely.

1599
01:03:34,720 --> 01:03:37,930
These are just some subsets
of type of these evaluation

1600
01:03:37,930 --> 01:03:40,300
instructions, but we
had over 60 of them.

1601
01:03:40,300 --> 01:03:43,000
We didn't do a full quantitative
ablation, for example,

1602
01:03:43,000 --> 01:03:44,590
as we did in RT-1.

1603
01:03:44,590 --> 01:03:47,110
We had this seen
and unseen task set,

1604
01:03:47,110 --> 01:03:48,550
and that was compositional.

1605
01:03:48,550 --> 01:03:51,272
You'd see, "move
coke near apple,"

1606
01:03:51,272 --> 01:03:53,230
and you would see, "move
apple in your sponge."

1607
01:03:53,230 --> 01:03:55,120
But we'd hold out "move
coke near sponge,"

1608
01:03:55,120 --> 01:03:56,078
and we'd test that out.

1609
01:03:56,078 --> 01:03:58,495
But in this case, I think we
can go much more beyond that.

1610
01:03:58,495 --> 01:04:00,760
Because our language is
completely free-form.

1611
01:04:00,760 --> 01:04:04,000
The compositional space
of what you can combine

1612
01:04:04,000 --> 01:04:05,420
is just going to be much larger.

1613
01:04:05,420 --> 01:04:06,545
So we did try a little bit.

1614
01:04:06,545 --> 01:04:08,020
To answer your
question, we tried

1615
01:04:08,020 --> 01:04:11,140
some combinatorial evaluations,
but there's definitely

1616
01:04:11,140 --> 01:04:15,300
a lot more thoroughness
that we could do there too.

1617
01:04:15,300 --> 01:04:16,530
How am I doing on time?

1618
01:04:16,530 --> 01:04:17,530
OK, 10 minutes.

1619
01:04:17,530 --> 01:04:20,130
Maybe I'll try to wrap
up pretty soon, then.

1620
01:04:20,130 --> 01:04:24,045
The DIAL Takeaway then is
that two parts, lesson 2,

1621
01:04:24,045 --> 01:04:25,170
leverage foundation models.

1622
01:04:25,170 --> 01:04:27,000
Let's use VLMs as
data augmentation.

1623
01:04:27,000 --> 01:04:30,240
And lesson 3, let's make sure
that our offline data set

1624
01:04:30,240 --> 01:04:33,720
is robust enough where these
different behaviors exist.

1625
01:04:33,720 --> 01:04:35,378
And you can describe
them in language.

1626
01:04:35,378 --> 01:04:37,170
If you don't have enough
diverse behaviors,

1627
01:04:37,170 --> 01:04:38,670
no matter how good
your labeling is,

1628
01:04:38,670 --> 01:04:41,160
you probably can't elicit all
of the interesting concepts

1629
01:04:41,160 --> 01:04:43,530
that you want to learn from.

1630
01:04:43,530 --> 01:04:45,960
And maybe most exciting for
me here was that, actually,

1631
01:04:45,960 --> 01:04:47,400
some label noise is OK.

1632
01:04:47,400 --> 01:04:49,620
Notoriously in supervised
learning and imitation

1633
01:04:49,620 --> 01:04:51,450
learning, you need
very clean labels

1634
01:04:51,450 --> 01:04:53,340
that are always 100% true.

1635
01:04:53,340 --> 01:04:56,620
You don't want to be learning
from noisy data where some--

1636
01:04:56,620 --> 01:04:58,900
a large percentage
is just not accurate.

1637
01:04:58,900 --> 01:05:03,120
But in our case, it seems
that some label noise was OK.

1638
01:05:03,120 --> 01:05:04,710
The vision language
model was not

1639
01:05:04,710 --> 01:05:08,280
always predicting factually
accurate descriptions

1640
01:05:08,280 --> 01:05:09,940
of the scene.

1641
01:05:09,940 --> 01:05:11,940
And I think this
definitely hurt when

1642
01:05:11,940 --> 01:05:13,380
it got too high, the noise.

1643
01:05:13,380 --> 01:05:15,300
But at smaller
levels, it definitely

1644
01:05:15,300 --> 01:05:20,470
still seemed to be OK and
robust enough to handle that.

1645
01:05:20,470 --> 01:05:23,800
So that was a deep dive then
on some individual works that

1646
01:05:23,800 --> 01:05:28,300
use this big recipe of language,
foundation models, offline data

1647
01:05:28,300 --> 01:05:31,550
sets in different parts
of the robot system.

1648
01:05:31,550 --> 01:05:34,190
And this was the pitch
at the beginning.

1649
01:05:34,190 --> 01:05:37,280
And I hope you at least see a
little bit of how our team has

1650
01:05:37,280 --> 01:05:40,040
tried to take these
principles and apply them

1651
01:05:40,040 --> 01:05:43,130
to accelerating robot
learning in the real world.

1652
01:05:43,130 --> 01:05:46,520
As we see, these different
types of ingredients and lessons

1653
01:05:46,520 --> 01:05:50,050
map onto different parts of
the robot system altogether.

1654
01:05:50,050 --> 01:05:51,050
But it's still learning.

1655
01:05:51,050 --> 01:05:52,670
That was RT-1 that
we talked about.

1656
01:05:52,670 --> 01:05:54,170
For planning, that was SayCan.

1657
01:05:54,170 --> 01:05:55,760
And then adding the
close of feedback

1658
01:05:55,760 --> 01:05:58,370
with vision language models,
that was inner monologue.

1659
01:05:58,370 --> 01:06:00,800
For low-level control, we
didn't talk about this today,

1660
01:06:00,800 --> 01:06:02,630
but exciting work from
our team is actually

1661
01:06:02,630 --> 01:06:04,820
using language models
to predict code

1662
01:06:04,820 --> 01:06:06,990
that's executed on the
robot directly, perhaps

1663
01:06:06,990 --> 01:06:08,828
as low-level controls.

1664
01:06:08,828 --> 01:06:10,370
Language models,
they read textbooks,

1665
01:06:10,370 --> 01:06:12,080
they read-- they've
read raw ROSS docs.

1666
01:06:12,080 --> 01:06:14,480
They've read UR5
documentation code.

1667
01:06:14,480 --> 01:06:16,370
And they can write
code for these robots,

1668
01:06:16,370 --> 01:06:18,200
and we can execute that.

1669
01:06:18,200 --> 01:06:20,060
For data augmentation,
we saw DIAL

1670
01:06:20,060 --> 01:06:21,650
with vision language models.

1671
01:06:21,650 --> 01:06:23,990
And also, I didn't
talk about this here,

1672
01:06:23,990 --> 01:06:26,450
but for object-centric
representations for things

1673
01:06:26,450 --> 01:06:29,060
like feature activation
maps for specific objects,

1674
01:06:29,060 --> 01:06:31,910
we can use those as
task representations

1675
01:06:31,910 --> 01:06:32,900
for mapping a scene.

1676
01:06:32,900 --> 01:06:37,340
And in NLMap, they did that
for object-centric navigation

1677
01:06:37,340 --> 01:06:39,980
around the micro kitchen
that we looked at.

1678
01:06:39,980 --> 01:06:43,520
And I think, hopefully, in the
next coming weeks and months,

1679
01:06:43,520 --> 01:06:46,740
we have a few more rows and
entries to add here as well.

1680
01:06:46,740 --> 01:06:50,990
But I think this kind of mindset
is a very exciting research

1681
01:06:50,990 --> 01:06:54,230
direction of how you can apply
these big high-level concepts

1682
01:06:54,230 --> 01:06:56,240
about foundation models
and offline data sets.

1683
01:06:56,240 --> 01:06:59,390
And you look at what exists
in the robot systems of today.

1684
01:06:59,390 --> 01:07:02,330
And you find many gaps in
opportunities still available,

1685
01:07:02,330 --> 01:07:05,600
where we can do everything
from exploratory pilots

1686
01:07:05,600 --> 01:07:07,940
on how this might look
all the way to more

1687
01:07:07,940 --> 01:07:10,580
extensive evaluations and really
building out robust systems.

1688
01:07:10,580 --> 01:07:13,480
I think both of
these have value.

1689
01:07:13,480 --> 01:07:16,500
So I'll conclude
with just saying

1690
01:07:16,500 --> 01:07:18,720
that it was very
fun exploring all

1691
01:07:18,720 --> 01:07:20,380
of these complementary
directions.

1692
01:07:20,380 --> 01:07:22,530
But there are still
some major questions

1693
01:07:22,530 --> 01:07:24,720
of how we can take these
concepts even further

1694
01:07:24,720 --> 01:07:26,700
in how these trends
and ideas might even

1695
01:07:26,700 --> 01:07:29,610
evolve moving forward as
foundation models get better,

1696
01:07:29,610 --> 01:07:32,130
as more data set becomes
available online,

1697
01:07:32,130 --> 01:07:34,890
as more data becomes
homogenized and tokenized,

1698
01:07:34,890 --> 01:07:35,855
and interoperable.

1699
01:07:35,855 --> 01:07:37,230
And I think a lot
of the concepts

1700
01:07:37,230 --> 01:07:40,080
from other fields like
linguistics and vision,

1701
01:07:40,080 --> 01:07:44,220
and from all of the big
scaling level questions

1702
01:07:44,220 --> 01:07:46,980
that are being pioneered in
language-based foundation

1703
01:07:46,980 --> 01:07:47,680
models.

1704
01:07:47,680 --> 01:07:50,490
Hopefully, those ideas can
trickle down to robotics.

1705
01:07:50,490 --> 01:07:52,470
Maybe even robotics can
provide something back

1706
01:07:52,470 --> 01:07:55,890
by providing embodied action
causal data sets that maybe

1707
01:07:55,890 --> 01:07:58,290
might improve the
quality of reasoning

1708
01:07:58,290 --> 01:08:01,710
of some of these large language
models that are not embodied.

1709
01:08:01,710 --> 01:08:05,460
With that, though, I guess
I'd like to thank everyone

1710
01:08:05,460 --> 01:08:06,720
for your time and for--

1711
01:08:06,720 --> 01:08:08,220
Dave and [INAUDIBLE]
for inviting me

1712
01:08:08,220 --> 01:08:11,940
and open to any questions about
the papers or just at a high

1713
01:08:11,940 --> 01:08:13,150
level as well.

1714
01:08:13,150 --> 01:08:13,860
Thanks so much.

